WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:05.171
[string instrumental]

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:37.537
♪♪♪

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:47.380
PROFESSOR WHEELER:
Blenheim Palace.

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:49.716
It's so interesting
that apparently Her Majesty,

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:51.259
whenever she goes
there, always says,

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:53.386
"Why is your Palace
bigger than mine?'

00:00:53.887 --> 00:01:00.518
♪♪♪

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.078
FRANK MANNION: We're at a
quintessentially British event,

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:19.662
Sir James,
what brings you here?

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:21.247
So this is an amazing place.

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:22.665
I've never been here before.

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:23.917
I've obviously
been to Blenheim,

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:26.002
but not to Salon Privé before.

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:28.922
This is the Duke
of Marlborough Award.

00:01:28.963 --> 00:01:31.091
So Your Grace,
would you like to present Ben

00:01:31.132 --> 00:01:32.258
with his award, please?

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.679
FRANK MANNION: Andrew,
we're at the Salon Privé.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:37.889
What brings you here?

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.642
We've been before,
we've enjoyed it, I like cars

00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:42.727
and it's a nice setting,
you know,

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.104
it's just a nice thing to do.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.356
What's special
about British cars?

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:47.774
When they work?

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.611
They have a personality,
a character.

00:01:52.821 --> 00:01:55.240
FRANK MANNION: What would you
say is the defining qualities

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.825
of being
quintessentially British?

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.953
Well, I think that
we're very friendly people.

00:01:59.994 --> 00:02:03.748
I think we're very relaxed
and creative and I suppose

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.416
a little bit quirky.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.460
And you think
of all the incredible things

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:08.962
that have come
out of this country,

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:12.090
from the Rolling Stones and
the Beatles to Aston Martin.

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:15.135
I mean, it's an absolutely
fantastic mélange

00:02:15.176 --> 00:02:17.387
of excitement
and creativity, I think.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.974
♪♪♪

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:22.767
FRANK MANNION: Our feature
documentary is called

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.185
&lt;i&gt;Quintessentially British.&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:27.355
What does quintessentially
British mean to you?

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:32.902
Unique, I think, probably is
the best way to describe it.

00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:37.115
It's very difficult
to pin down

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:39.701
what it means to be British.

00:02:40.118 --> 00:02:43.454
There is something
about self-deprecation,

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:48.168
something about attachment
to history and tradition.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:49.919
Quiet pride.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.673
Being in a garden,
a green space.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.303
I think of our British
sense of humor is really

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.763
what the British people turn to,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:04.684
in times of trouble,
and what helps us get out of it.

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:07.520
I think the way we speak,

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.731
because we're full of irony,

00:03:10.231 --> 00:03:11.941
so ironic and always saying

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:13.985
things we don't quite mean.

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:16.154
There's always "Well, what did
he mean when he said that?"

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.947
There's always that element.
I love that.

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:20.325
Politeness, kindness.

00:03:21.618 --> 00:03:23.119
Stuck-up-ness.

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:25.872
When American friends
of mine come over here,

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:27.624
they often say,
"Oh you know, the English

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.459
are so friendly." And I think

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:32.545
to myself and I sometimes say
to them, "They're civil."

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:36.090
They're not really
necessarily friendly.

00:03:36.174 --> 00:03:39.219
But you know, they're polite
in a way that you know,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.139
in America you go
into a shop or the supermarket

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:44.682
and you know,
they're all over you

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:46.226
with this kind of,
you know, bonhomie,

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:48.144
which then is just switched off.

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:50.355
You know,
we don't overstate things.

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.857
Americans are all about these
big gestures, aren't they?

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:54.651
You know,
when they meet each other

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.736
and they're sort of "Oh,
it's fantastic to see you."

00:03:56.778 --> 00:03:59.072
We're a little bit quieter,
you know, sort of assume

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:00.698
that it's good to see you,

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.283
but we'd really
just rather sort of get

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:05.245
down to talking
about what's going on.

00:04:05.286 --> 00:04:07.997
We do like to go our own way
and do our own thing,

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:11.709
and listening to other people
can be difficult at times.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.586
Yes.

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:15.838
We kind of have a strict sense
of formal etiquette.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.924
But in truth,
anyone who's spent

00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:19.717
any time with normal
British people would know

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:22.303
that we're basically a lot
of the time drunk.

00:04:22.804 --> 00:04:24.722
So, we're a mass
of contradictions.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.474
NEIL ROBINSON: I think
if you ask my wife

00:04:26.557 --> 00:04:28.268
what the quintessential
characteristics

00:04:28.309 --> 00:04:30.353
of an Englishmen are,
she would say good manners,

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:33.356
respectful behaviour,
decent standards of dress.

00:04:34.065 --> 00:04:36.192
Perhaps she's a little old
fashioned in that.

00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:38.194
Just being very English.

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:39.737
[laughs]

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:46.911
If the cliché of the Englishman
is his politeness,

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:51.291
his discretion,
his privacy, obeys the rules,

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:55.295
is it any wonder
that those same people

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:57.797
love going to the theatre
where everything is different

00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:00.967
and the people let their hair
down and sometimes their pants.

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:09.475
We love the theatre,
because so many of us are

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:11.894
not theatrical in our lives.

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:17.066
And the most famous Englishman
who ever lived wrote plays,

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.234
but was an actor.

00:05:20.611 --> 00:05:21.654
Not a king.

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:23.656
Not a politician.

00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:25.325
Not an athlete.

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:27.493
Not a saint.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:33.666
Our national hero is an actor.

00:05:34.417 --> 00:05:37.003
Of course, other cultures
love their theater.

00:05:37.045 --> 00:05:40.673
And everyone loves storytelling
it's a very, very basic need.

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:44.177
But the Brits have caught on
to it, perhaps more than most.

00:05:45.386 --> 00:05:47.555
We've been at it a long time.

00:05:48.973 --> 00:05:51.017
FRANK MANNION: Just do a sound
check, Ian, just--

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.643
Yes, yes, go ahead. Yup.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:54.020
FRANK MANNION: Sir Ian McKellen.

00:05:54.896 --> 00:05:57.857
You-- please, I'm not Sir.

00:05:57.899 --> 00:05:59.317
FRANK MANNION: Okay, okay.

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:00.860
That's a title.

00:06:00.902 --> 00:06:02.612
It's not a name.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:04.405
Unless you're calling
everybody else Mister.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.159
FRANK MANNION: Sure.
Okay. Ian McKellen.

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.411
Yep, yep, thank you.

00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:16.501
I began to like Shakespeare
by going to see his plays.

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:17.710
Not by reading them.

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:19.337
Or being taught them.

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:21.214
He knew, it seems better

00:06:21.255 --> 00:06:23.091
than any other writer
who ever lived,

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:26.469
the variety of human needs
and passions,

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:27.804
and desires.

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.555
That's the basis of it all

00:06:29.597 --> 00:06:33.851
is the jealousy, the love,
the hatred, the fear.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:36.979
OZ CLARKE: Shakespeare,
in one speech tells you

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:43.820
more about the life
of a man, from cradle to grave

00:06:44.112 --> 00:06:48.199
than a thousand pages
of someone else's writing

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:50.034
could tell you.

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:52.995
We love tales of horror
and violence and murder

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:54.831
and people doing terrible
things to each other.

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:56.833
And Shakespeare does
them so brilliantly.

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:59.877
IAN MACKELLAN: He only
invented one plot for himself

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:01.587
in the whole of the 37 plays.

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:02.922
That was the last play he wrote.

00:07:02.964 --> 00:07:04.340
All the others he pinched.

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.050
What? From history books,

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:08.344
from poetry books,
from other people's plays.

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:10.680
There was a Hamlet play
before Shakespeare wrote his.

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:14.767
We filmed Hamlet before
we put it onto the stage

00:07:14.809 --> 00:07:17.854
and when we were filming it,
the Duke of Edinburgh died.

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:22.233
The Duke of Edinburgh dying
as we were filming a play

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:25.736
about a royal family
who lived in a castle.

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:28.030
And here we were in
the shadow of Windsor Castle,

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:29.449
and we were
filming the castle.

00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:32.452
You know, the queen losing
her husband after so long.

00:07:32.535 --> 00:07:34.620
Here we are doing a story
about a queen who

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:36.789
loses a husband and marries
her husband's brother.

00:07:36.831 --> 00:07:39.625
It's hard not to see what
the connections are.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:43.963
Ian McKellen was wary
of playing Hamlet

00:07:44.005 --> 00:07:48.050
because he's 80 and Hamlet's 20.

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:52.054
But I thought he can respect
the language and the poetry,

00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:55.433
but still make it his own
and make it modern.

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:58.978
Because whilst he's known
now as Gandalf, he's still

00:07:59.061 --> 00:08:01.731
sort of known
for being this great,

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:03.608
great Shakespearean actor.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:07.153
Oh, we're living in an age
when an 82 year old man

00:08:07.195 --> 00:08:09.906
is pretending
to be a 22 year old boy

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:12.325
and that specific man
doing it, MacKellen.

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:14.619
Here we are, it's live.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:17.246
And never forget,
you can never forget

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:18.831
when you're seeing
a Shakespeare play

00:08:18.873 --> 00:08:20.208
that that's exactly
what you're doing.

00:08:20.917 --> 00:08:22.335
You're seeing a play.

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:25.046
REPORTER: Dame Judi.

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:26.756
REPORTER 2: Dame Judi,
to your left please.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.133
REPORTER 3:
Over here please, Judi.

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:30.927
FRANK MANNION: We were filming
with Sir Ian McKellen

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.347
with Hamlet and you of course,
played Ophelia in Hamlet

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:36.015
-and Hamlet--
-And Gertrude.

00:08:36.098 --> 00:08:37.350
FRANK MANNION:
And Gertrude and Ophelia--

00:08:37.391 --> 00:08:39.018
Not in the same production.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:40.478
FRANK MANNION:
Not in the same production.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.188
What is it
about Shakespeare that is

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:43.523
so quintessentially English

00:08:43.606 --> 00:08:44.732
and beloved around the world?

00:08:44.815 --> 00:08:46.359
I have two passions.

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.778
One is Shakespeare.
The other is trees.

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:51.864
And I was brought up
on Shakespeare as a little girl,

00:08:51.906 --> 00:08:53.950
and my family used to quote it.

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:57.954
Reams of it, I could do
a whole play for you now.

00:08:58.454 --> 00:09:01.791
If you like the&lt;i&gt; Dream&lt;/i&gt; or&lt;i&gt; Twelfth&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;Night,&lt;/i&gt; I could do all that.

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:03.793
I could do a lot
of&lt;i&gt; Measure for Measure.&lt;/i&gt;

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:06.045
I could do a bit
of&lt;i&gt; The Merchant of Venice.&lt;/i&gt;

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:09.966
It's a good note
to give to tired old actors

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:12.218
of Shakespeare is, you know

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.761
Shakespeare was an Italian?

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:18.891
"To be or not to be,
that's the question," I mean--

00:09:20.393 --> 00:09:24.313
Italians, the cliche for us
who are not Italians,

00:09:24.397 --> 00:09:25.898
is that they never stop talking.

00:09:26.524 --> 00:09:27.900
You can say
the same of the Irish.

00:09:29.569 --> 00:09:31.362
And talking very well.

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:35.283
Good talk. Not idle chatter.

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:40.997
But done with great flair
and imagination and metaphor

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:46.419
and a variety of pace
in the speaking and the hands

00:09:46.502 --> 00:09:48.838
and the gestures
coming to it in a way

00:09:48.921 --> 00:09:53.884
that the British wouldn't
express themselves these days.

00:09:53.926 --> 00:09:56.345
I mean, we've had
the Victorian era to live

00:09:56.429 --> 00:10:00.516
through to change
our collective personality,

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.352
our national personality.
But I think Shakespeare's

00:10:03.436 --> 00:10:08.482
characters are much more what
we think of as Italian or Irish.

00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:14.780
[jaunty keyboard instrumental]

00:10:16.032 --> 00:10:18.993
EMMA ELWICK-BATES: My world
at the moment, we talk so much

00:10:19.076 --> 00:10:22.163
about the provenance
of where your item is made.

00:10:23.331 --> 00:10:25.791
And I think what's
so interesting about the Queen,

00:10:25.875 --> 00:10:28.169
but also
the Royal Family at large,

00:10:28.252 --> 00:10:30.296
is they've
always shopped that way.

00:10:30.379 --> 00:10:33.841
They've always supported
the Savile Row's,

00:10:33.924 --> 00:10:35.926
the Henry Poole's,
Prince Charles gets

00:10:35.968 --> 00:10:38.554
his John Lobb shoes,
the Duke of Edinburgh,

00:10:38.638 --> 00:10:40.473
a Lock &amp; Co. cap.

00:10:40.514 --> 00:10:44.727
And that's so important
to them to support these brands

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:47.313
and to give them their Royal
Warrants and I think

00:10:47.396 --> 00:10:51.609
it's often underestimated
how important that is

00:10:51.651 --> 00:10:53.944
and how that keeps
these businesses alive.

00:10:54.695 --> 00:10:58.658
I have to confess that
I have never, ever been able

00:10:58.699 --> 00:11:03.245
to afford to go to Savile Row
to buy my clothes.

00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:08.751
But I think there is something
about precision tailoring,

00:11:08.834 --> 00:11:12.755
genuine craftsmanship
that is an important

00:11:12.838 --> 00:11:16.384
bit of British making tradition.

00:11:16.467 --> 00:11:18.928
LADY CARNARVON: The sartorial
elegance, which is actually

00:11:19.011 --> 00:11:21.597
partly seen in Downton Abbey,
is very British,

00:11:21.681 --> 00:11:23.557
whether it's
through James Bond

00:11:23.641 --> 00:11:24.975
or the Savile Row tailors.

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:26.686
I think the British
are known for it

00:11:26.727 --> 00:11:28.562
and it's trying
to set a high bar.

00:11:28.646 --> 00:11:30.898
And some ways, of course,
that's what His Royal Highness

00:11:30.981 --> 00:11:32.775
Prince Philip set out to do.

00:11:32.858 --> 00:11:36.195
He always encouraged people
to be the best

00:11:36.237 --> 00:11:38.280
and that's
where you should aim for.

00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:42.034
Not half tying a tie,
but tying a tie really well.

00:11:42.076 --> 00:11:45.204
Learning how to do something
really well, you know,

00:11:45.246 --> 00:11:47.665
looking at someone's shoes,
are they polished or not?

00:11:47.707 --> 00:11:50.543
And I still find I look
at someone's shoes,

00:11:50.584 --> 00:11:53.045
just glancing down
just seeing whether

00:11:53.087 --> 00:11:54.880
they're polished or not.

00:11:55.548 --> 00:11:59.218
My husband gave our son
a 21st birthday present

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.053
of a suit from Savile Row.

00:12:01.512 --> 00:12:05.391
So it's that wonderful process
of choosing the fabric,

00:12:05.474 --> 00:12:08.436
of deciding, it's of getting
measured, of getting adjusted.

00:12:08.519 --> 00:12:13.858
That whole process and journey
is a delight to watch.

00:12:14.817 --> 00:12:16.736
There is no other
street like Savile Row.

00:12:16.777 --> 00:12:19.530
Through the generations it's
survived and it's survived

00:12:19.572 --> 00:12:21.490
because of obviously the great
houses, the names that have

00:12:21.574 --> 00:12:23.367
existed here on the Row.

00:12:23.409 --> 00:12:26.871
We don't claim to be
in fashion at any one time,

00:12:26.912 --> 00:12:28.372
because fashion comes and goes.

00:12:28.414 --> 00:12:30.166
Like the tide,
it comes in and out.

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:33.252
Bespoke is a tailoring term
and the young will

00:12:33.294 --> 00:12:35.921
always have a need
to create things

00:12:35.963 --> 00:12:37.173
that are unique for them.

00:12:39.258 --> 00:12:41.218
SIMON CUNDEY: Henry Poole
really is the founding fathers

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:44.430
of Savile Row and also the great
inventors of the dinner suit,

00:12:44.472 --> 00:12:45.931
otherwise known as the Tuxedo.

00:12:46.557 --> 00:12:49.602
It's a great honour to be
at Henry Poole and being known

00:12:49.643 --> 00:12:51.937
for dressing some
of the great names of the past,

00:12:51.979 --> 00:12:54.774
such as Vanderbilts,
such as J.P. Morgan,

00:12:54.815 --> 00:12:57.902
such as Napoleon III,
such as the Tzars of Russia,

00:12:57.943 --> 00:12:59.570
even the Emperor of Japan.

00:12:59.612 --> 00:13:01.614
Still today, these names stand.

00:13:02.948 --> 00:13:05.534
Winston Churchill was probably
one of our great men

00:13:05.618 --> 00:13:07.411
that have walked in here.

00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:10.164
And of course, he came to us
as a young, slim gentleman

00:13:10.247 --> 00:13:13.918
and got to meet my great
grandfather and my grandfather.

00:13:13.959 --> 00:13:17.129
And my grandfather remembered
talking about his suits

00:13:17.213 --> 00:13:19.965
that came in for abundant
alteration cleaning

00:13:20.007 --> 00:13:21.634
due to the cigar smoke.

00:13:21.675 --> 00:13:23.260
And they would always find
little bits of ash

00:13:23.636 --> 00:13:25.221
and stubs inside pockets,

00:13:25.304 --> 00:13:27.306
which was very much
his character.

00:13:27.807 --> 00:13:30.059
But he was apparently
a wonderful man,

00:13:30.142 --> 00:13:31.644
even though
he was quite moody

00:13:31.685 --> 00:13:33.187
at some points,
you know, and we did

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:34.980
have a little
tit for tats over bills

00:13:35.022 --> 00:13:36.857
that were eventually sorted.

00:13:37.691 --> 00:13:39.401
This is what
we call measure books.

00:13:42.279 --> 00:13:44.782
So we would have
measured Winston's father,

00:13:44.824 --> 00:13:48.244
and this is 1886
by the looks of it.

00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:53.290
Later on in life, Winston came
to us in 1906, which is there,

00:13:53.833 --> 00:13:56.168
and that's some
of his clothes that was made.

00:13:57.837 --> 00:14:00.965
You'll always meet customers
and get to know them,

00:14:01.006 --> 00:14:02.508
which is the best part
of the business.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:04.760
How would you
like your pockets here?

00:14:04.844 --> 00:14:06.178
This is the big question here.

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:08.430
Well, generally
I don't use them.

00:14:08.514 --> 00:14:10.850
You know, I won't be putting
any cigars in them.

00:14:10.933 --> 00:14:12.601
-Like Churchill.
-You should, you should.

00:14:12.685 --> 00:14:14.395
-I should.
-Four working buttons

00:14:14.478 --> 00:14:16.105
on the cuff? So there's
four working buttons here?

00:14:16.188 --> 00:14:17.356
Yes please, yes.

00:14:17.398 --> 00:14:18.649
Pleats or no pleats?

00:14:18.691 --> 00:14:19.900
That's a very good question.

00:14:19.984 --> 00:14:22.152
You're not strong on the thigh,

00:14:22.194 --> 00:14:23.571
so you don't
need to have pleats.

00:14:23.654 --> 00:14:25.698
-Right.
-Zip fly or button fly?

00:14:25.739 --> 00:14:27.366
-Zip fly.
-I have to ask.

00:14:27.408 --> 00:14:30.411
-Yeah.
-But for most people it's zip.

00:14:30.703 --> 00:14:34.874
So, single-breasted,
button two, notched lapel.

00:14:34.915 --> 00:14:38.085
So, that's your plain
weave with no colorway.

00:14:38.168 --> 00:14:41.380
That's your pinhead,
showing the actual distances

00:14:41.463 --> 00:14:42.840
between the two colorways.

00:14:43.507 --> 00:14:45.968
And I probably would
put it between those two.

00:14:46.552 --> 00:14:50.055
So, Simon, I think executive
decision, I think let's go...

00:14:50.097 --> 00:14:51.515
-Yes.
-Let's go for this.

00:14:51.557 --> 00:14:52.808
Yes, I think
that's a lovely one.

00:14:52.892 --> 00:14:54.518
It perfectly works well.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.353
It's smart casual
and dress it up, dress it down.

00:14:56.395 --> 00:14:58.063
And also it has
a bit more character,

00:14:58.105 --> 00:14:59.732
it has a little bit
more of the depth

00:14:59.773 --> 00:15:01.025
in the marbling effect
with the pinhead.

00:15:01.066 --> 00:15:02.568
So it's a good, good choice.

00:15:03.235 --> 00:15:05.154
So when you mention Savile Row,
I immediately think

00:15:05.237 --> 00:15:07.197
of The Beatles
and their last live performance

00:15:07.239 --> 00:15:09.408
on the roof at number 3,
Savile Row.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.828
Sadly a bit
before my time on the row.

00:15:12.912 --> 00:15:14.955
There's quite
often tours that come,

00:15:15.039 --> 00:15:17.791
they will always stop
outside the Apple building,

00:15:17.875 --> 00:15:20.836
before they come and stop here
at number 10, Savile Row.

00:15:22.087 --> 00:15:25.299
We're very fortunate,
we have quite an eclectic

00:15:25.382 --> 00:15:26.926
range of customers.

00:15:26.967 --> 00:15:30.429
Officers in the army,
to captains of industry.

00:15:30.471 --> 00:15:33.307
And we have been known
to make the odd outfit

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:34.975
for heads of state.

00:15:35.684 --> 00:15:37.937
Thankfully,
I've still got my head.

00:15:39.939 --> 00:15:42.691
Prince William
and Prince Harry did their year

00:15:42.775 --> 00:15:45.110
at Sandhurst and whilst
they were training there,

00:15:45.194 --> 00:15:47.780
we were asked
to make their initial uniforms.

00:15:48.447 --> 00:15:51.075
We received a great
accolade and the fact

00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:54.286
that their first official
portrait was of them

00:15:54.328 --> 00:15:57.498
wearing their uniforms
made by Dege &amp; Skinner.

00:15:58.624 --> 00:16:00.960
When Prince Harry married,
we were approached

00:16:01.043 --> 00:16:03.712
and asked if we would
make the frock-coat

00:16:03.796 --> 00:16:06.048
uniform for the Blues
and Royals,

00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:07.925
which was his mother regiment.

00:16:07.967 --> 00:16:09.718
Other than the people
that were measuring

00:16:09.802 --> 00:16:12.221
and doing the fitting,
no one actually knew

00:16:12.304 --> 00:16:13.722
who we were making it for.

00:16:15.724 --> 00:16:18.102
We're going to be making you
a sports coat,

00:16:18.143 --> 00:16:19.979
but a sports coat
with a difference.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:23.857
This jacket here is what
we call the Bentley coat.

00:16:24.358 --> 00:16:28.487
We've utilised the Bentley
stitching that is seen

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:30.781
on every car steering wheel.

00:16:30.823 --> 00:16:34.910
And I understand that it
takes 50 man hours to make

00:16:34.994 --> 00:16:36.495
a Bentley steering wheel.

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:38.914
Which is, funnily enough,
the same period

00:16:38.998 --> 00:16:41.417
of time it takes us
to make a two piece suit,

00:16:41.875 --> 00:16:43.502
50 to 60 man hours.

00:16:44.086 --> 00:16:49.299
The buttons are an engraved
Bentley wheel nut that would

00:16:49.341 --> 00:16:51.176
have been on all
the original Bentley's.

00:16:51.218 --> 00:16:52.678
Get the rubber hammer out,

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.847
bang it, untwist it,
wheel comes off.

00:16:54.888 --> 00:16:56.432
Bit like Formula One,

00:16:56.515 --> 00:16:59.059
but not as quick
as Formula One these days.

00:17:00.477 --> 00:17:07.234
♪♪♪

00:17:10.529 --> 00:17:14.450
My full name is
Adrian Bailie Nottage Palmer

00:17:14.533 --> 00:17:17.202
and I am the 4th Baron
of Reading.

00:17:17.244 --> 00:17:19.038
And I'm terribly fortunate

00:17:19.496 --> 00:17:22.750
that I am able to wear quite
a lot of my father's clothes

00:17:22.833 --> 00:17:25.044
and indeed
my grandfather's clothes.

00:17:25.127 --> 00:17:27.379
When I got married,
I actually wore

00:17:27.463 --> 00:17:29.339
my grandfather's tailcoat,
which was made

00:17:29.381 --> 00:17:31.717
in Savile Row in 1921.

00:17:33.177 --> 00:17:36.430
Huntley &amp; Palmers
was founded by Thomas Huntley.

00:17:36.513 --> 00:17:40.142
The Palmers and the Huntleys
were in fact related

00:17:40.809 --> 00:17:44.146
and as things took off,
he wrote to his cousin

00:17:44.229 --> 00:17:46.231
and said,
"Will you come and join me?"

00:17:46.273 --> 00:17:49.026
Huntley &amp; Palmers
had a Royal Warrant

00:17:49.068 --> 00:17:53.155
from every royal family
in the world.

00:17:53.739 --> 00:17:55.407
Beat that.

00:17:55.491 --> 00:17:57.910
I was the last member
of the family to do

00:17:57.993 --> 00:18:00.079
what they called
a really old fashioned

00:18:00.162 --> 00:18:02.539
apprenticeship and I always
remember when I went

00:18:02.581 --> 00:18:05.000
to the Huntley &amp; Palmer
factory in Huyton,

00:18:05.084 --> 00:18:08.212
they didn't like the idea of the
boss's son coming up here.

00:18:08.253 --> 00:18:11.673
And my first job was to get
all the chewing gum off

00:18:11.757 --> 00:18:13.592
the floor of the lady's loo.

00:18:13.634 --> 00:18:16.095
I swear to this day
that that lavatory

00:18:16.178 --> 00:18:17.846
had never been so clean.

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:19.640
They thought I would
last about 5 minutes,

00:18:19.723 --> 00:18:22.059
whereas in fact it took
me about 5 hours to do.

00:18:23.268 --> 00:18:26.897
I think I'm very fortunate
because I've had my biscuit

00:18:26.939 --> 00:18:30.067
life and then
I've had my farming life.

00:18:30.109 --> 00:18:33.821
I was 39 when I inherited
the title from my uncle.

00:18:33.904 --> 00:18:36.323
I always slightly assumed
that I would have inherited

00:18:36.406 --> 00:18:41.662
the title from my father, but
sadly he died before he was 71.

00:18:44.623 --> 00:18:47.751
I nearly always actually have
a bite to eat at lunch time

00:18:47.793 --> 00:18:50.838
in the staff restaurant where
the food is frightfully good

00:18:50.921 --> 00:18:53.173
and quite a bit cheaper
than upstairs.

00:18:53.632 --> 00:18:57.469
[suspenseful
string instrumental]

00:19:02.683 --> 00:19:04.518
FRANK MANNION: Do you
think the House of Lords

00:19:04.601 --> 00:19:05.978
-should be abolished?
-Definitely.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:08.981
The House of Commons, I think,
is the most incredible

00:19:09.022 --> 00:19:10.649
institution on the planet.

00:19:10.691 --> 00:19:12.276
The Lords...

00:19:12.734 --> 00:19:16.321
Anything hereditary without
actually doing it yourself

00:19:16.405 --> 00:19:18.824
and achieving it yourself
shouldn't be around.

00:19:18.866 --> 00:19:21.869
I don't see why, you know, you
should get there through birth.

00:19:21.952 --> 00:19:23.328
I think that's just ridiculous.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:25.289
It's a bit outdated,
if you will.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:27.791
So I've heard the House
of Lords be called

00:19:27.833 --> 00:19:30.294
the most expensive
daycare facility

00:19:30.335 --> 00:19:32.838
for the elderly,
with their subsidised meals

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:34.173
paid by the taxpayer.

00:19:34.256 --> 00:19:35.841
But that's all okay,
I mean,

00:19:35.883 --> 00:19:37.426
we have
the House of Commons, right?

00:19:37.509 --> 00:19:39.344
[laughing]

00:19:39.386 --> 00:19:41.513
I thought we were just going
to talk about some gardens here.

00:19:41.555 --> 00:19:43.056
I haven't studied it
in great depth,

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:44.850
to be honest,
but it probably could

00:19:44.892 --> 00:19:46.435
do with a bit
of modernisation.

00:19:46.768 --> 00:19:49.897
But I quite like the décor,
so don't modernize that.

00:19:51.899 --> 00:19:53.901
I'm quite keen
on the House of Lords.

00:19:53.984 --> 00:19:56.945
People want to abolish it, but
basically it could be renamed

00:19:57.029 --> 00:19:58.655
a House of Experts.

00:19:58.697 --> 00:20:01.325
Somebody once said
to my children's mother

00:20:01.366 --> 00:20:05.162
that I speak on every subject.
That is not true.

00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:10.000
If, for example, there was a
big division on a health bill

00:20:10.042 --> 00:20:14.254
amendment, I probably
wouldn't vote unless I'd spoken

00:20:14.338 --> 00:20:18.050
to somebody
who had health knowledge.

00:20:19.134 --> 00:20:21.011
FRANK MANNION: In the 30 years
that you've been here,

00:20:21.053 --> 00:20:23.013
what are you most proud of?

00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:26.308
Being able to participate,
particularly on amendments

00:20:26.391 --> 00:20:30.229
to various bills, being able
to change the course of history.

00:20:31.438 --> 00:20:35.234
I think you famously described
Manderston, your home,

00:20:35.317 --> 00:20:38.403
as it has 109 rooms,
but how many rooms too many?

00:20:38.445 --> 00:20:39.696
A hundred.

00:20:39.738 --> 00:20:41.740
[laughing]
Yes, so funny.

00:20:42.908 --> 00:20:46.370
First thing you get given when
you come here is a clothes peg

00:20:46.411 --> 00:20:50.165
and then, if we're lucky,
a locker and then even luckier

00:20:50.249 --> 00:20:52.084
still, a desk
and I've actually kept

00:20:52.125 --> 00:20:56.296
my great-grandfather's
nameplate,

00:20:56.713 --> 00:20:57.923
which I'm very proud of.

00:21:00.801 --> 00:21:02.886
SIR LINDSAY HOYLE:
This is a World Heritage site,

00:21:02.928 --> 00:21:06.682
this is an important building,
this is a working museum.

00:21:06.765 --> 00:21:08.976
It is the people's parliament.

00:21:09.059 --> 00:21:11.770
It is for the future
of this country.

00:21:11.812 --> 00:21:13.730
And I believe in this building.

00:21:13.772 --> 00:21:16.942
I believe in the fabric
of this building and I believe

00:21:16.984 --> 00:21:20.112
in democracy and this is
the home of democracy.

00:21:21.488 --> 00:21:23.115
Which is the Commons,
which is the Lords?

00:21:23.198 --> 00:21:25.242
Well, the good thing
is we always know,

00:21:25.284 --> 00:21:27.744
if you're at this end of the
building, everything's green.

00:21:27.786 --> 00:21:29.705
If you go
to the other end, it's red.

00:21:29.788 --> 00:21:32.457
So we have that bit
in the middle of no man's land.

00:21:33.542 --> 00:21:35.752
Do I look in envy to the Lords?

00:21:35.794 --> 00:21:37.796
I've got to say, it
might be a little bit grander.

00:21:37.838 --> 00:21:40.841
It might have a little bit
more gold gilding than we have.

00:21:40.924 --> 00:21:43.677
But the reality is,
we are the elected House,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:46.096
we are the democratic House.

00:21:46.138 --> 00:21:49.891
This is supremacy when it
comes between the two Houses.

00:21:50.892 --> 00:21:53.478
Democracy's never perfect,
we will all find fault,

00:21:53.562 --> 00:21:56.189
but if you're going to find one
democracy that people want

00:21:56.273 --> 00:22:00.694
to copy, it's this one and can I
stand by what I represent here?

00:22:00.777 --> 00:22:02.404
Absolutely.

00:22:02.487 --> 00:22:05.240
Am I proud
of the Westminster democracy?

00:22:05.324 --> 00:22:09.161
Yes, of course
and I'm so pleased there are

00:22:09.202 --> 00:22:11.413
so many parliaments
around the world

00:22:11.496 --> 00:22:14.541
that's based on this democracy.

00:22:14.624 --> 00:22:17.836
And that matters to me
and it matters the role

00:22:17.878 --> 00:22:19.504
that we played
around the world

00:22:19.546 --> 00:22:21.840
in ensuring democracy
comes first.

00:22:22.090 --> 00:22:25.677
The Westminster model,
I think by far, yes,

00:22:25.761 --> 00:22:28.513
I'm going to say, it's
the best model that we've got.

00:22:29.806 --> 00:22:33.518
Part of my family,
is not only my wife, daughter

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:36.688
and the grandchildren, but
it's also the pets, isn't it?

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:39.649
You know, Boris the parrot
thinks he's a politician.

00:22:39.691 --> 00:22:41.318
He loves coming to Parliament.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:43.487
He shouts, "Order,
order, lock the doors."

00:22:43.528 --> 00:22:45.572
Betty, a Patterdale Terrier,

00:22:45.655 --> 00:22:48.492
who wants to keep charge
of everything and of course,

00:22:48.533 --> 00:22:50.535
I've got Maggie the tortoise
and why's she called Maggie?

00:22:51.203 --> 00:22:53.705
She's not for turning
and she's got a hard shell.

00:22:53.789 --> 00:22:55.165
So of course as you realize,

00:22:55.207 --> 00:22:57.626
all the pets are
named after politicians.

00:22:57.709 --> 00:22:59.669
They all are part
of the family,

00:22:59.711 --> 00:23:02.589
they play the role
and quite rightly,

00:23:02.672 --> 00:23:04.633
they don't miss out either.

00:23:09.554 --> 00:23:12.891
LORD CHRIS SMITH: I've always
thought that I had the great

00:23:12.933 --> 00:23:17.896
advantage of growing up
as a teenager in the 1960s.

00:23:17.938 --> 00:23:22.109
I came out of that period
wanting to change the world.

00:23:23.068 --> 00:23:28.824
And I decided that democracy
was the way to achieve change.

00:23:30.409 --> 00:23:33.578
I wanted to come to Cambridge,
so I applied.

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:38.750
I was absolutely terrified.
I did a terrible interview,

00:23:38.792 --> 00:23:42.587
but remarkably, Pembroke
College gave me a place.

00:23:44.881 --> 00:23:50.053
Spending a really wonderful
period of time here,

00:23:50.095 --> 00:23:53.098
burying myself
in English literature

00:23:53.181 --> 00:23:57.602
was not just a treat,
but it was a rather

00:23:57.644 --> 00:23:59.396
wonderful preparation for life.

00:24:02.232 --> 00:24:04.901
One of the things
that inspired me

00:24:04.943 --> 00:24:09.448
in Cambridge was a supervision
with one of the Fellows.

00:24:09.531 --> 00:24:12.451
We had written essays, they
weren't terribly good essays.

00:24:12.492 --> 00:24:16.621
He said, "Let me tell you
what Wordsworth and Coleridge

00:24:16.663 --> 00:24:18.290
were really all about."

00:24:18.623 --> 00:24:22.878
And for the next 2 hours,
he paced up and down,

00:24:22.961 --> 00:24:24.421
pulled books out
of the bookcase

00:24:24.463 --> 00:24:25.964
and read quotations at us.

00:24:26.047 --> 00:24:30.552
He conjured up
a whole world for us.

00:24:30.635 --> 00:24:34.723
And that is the power
of a good supervision.

00:24:34.806 --> 00:24:38.810
It can inspire,
it can fire your imagination,

00:24:38.852 --> 00:24:42.898
it can fire your passion.
That transference

00:24:42.981 --> 00:24:48.695
of enthusiasm and inspiration
is what Cambridge teaching,

00:24:48.778 --> 00:24:51.990
at its very best,
is all about.

00:24:53.116 --> 00:24:56.077
It's no accident
that Cambridge has produced

00:24:56.161 --> 00:25:00.499
over 100 Nobel Prize winners
because of the quality

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.167
of intellectual attainment,

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:05.587
the depth of research,
the quality of teaching.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:09.883
If we lost that,
we would be diminished terribly.

00:25:09.966 --> 00:25:14.763
The ability to offer
to the very best and brightest

00:25:14.846 --> 00:25:18.350
of our students,
here in Britain,

00:25:18.391 --> 00:25:21.019
an opportunity
to flourish and to shine.

00:25:24.022 --> 00:25:26.483
IAN MACKELLAN: I was
at Cambridge on a scholarship,

00:25:26.525 --> 00:25:28.443
so I didn't get
there through privilege.

00:25:28.527 --> 00:25:30.445
But once you're there,
you realise that you're

00:25:30.529 --> 00:25:33.657
part of a great deal
of historical privilege.

00:25:33.698 --> 00:25:36.451
If I hadn't been to Cambridge,
perhaps I wouldn't have been

00:25:36.535 --> 00:25:38.954
invited to go to the Royal
Shakespeare Company

00:25:39.037 --> 00:25:41.373
by these Cambridge people
who were running it.

00:25:41.957 --> 00:25:43.917
I went for the interview
and I didn't get in.

00:25:44.292 --> 00:25:46.211
And I think they saw
that I wasn't properly posh.

00:25:46.294 --> 00:25:48.547
That's why.
And I was just talking a lot.

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.757
And I think they were like,
"Oh, please."

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:52.300
I'm not bitter, though.
I'm definitely not bitter

00:25:52.384 --> 00:25:53.635
about it,
I never think about it,

00:25:53.718 --> 00:25:54.886
even though it's 20 years ago.

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:59.224
My experience of Cambridge
was that I flourished

00:25:59.266 --> 00:26:00.684
because I went there to work.

00:26:01.476 --> 00:26:02.852
I loved it.

00:26:02.894 --> 00:26:04.646
It was very high pressure.

00:26:04.729 --> 00:26:08.108
Now, a lot of students
couldn't take the pressure,

00:26:08.191 --> 00:26:09.568
wouldn't enjoy the pressure,

00:26:09.609 --> 00:26:10.902
would hate the pressure.

00:26:11.736 --> 00:26:14.364
I'm Callum Sullivan
and I recently graduated

00:26:14.406 --> 00:26:16.241
with a degree in music.

00:26:16.324 --> 00:26:18.577
I captained my Cambridge crew
in the Boat Race

00:26:18.618 --> 00:26:20.245
against Oxford to victory.

00:26:20.704 --> 00:26:23.290
When I really decided
I wanted to apply to Cambridge

00:26:23.373 --> 00:26:25.250
and put in the work
that meant I could

00:26:25.292 --> 00:26:27.752
get there was actually
probably watching a Boat Race.

00:26:28.253 --> 00:26:30.422
I realised at that point
that this was an event

00:26:30.505 --> 00:26:31.923
that was really special

00:26:31.965 --> 00:26:33.883
and that I actually
had the capacity

00:26:33.925 --> 00:26:36.386
to possibly
be in this environment.

00:26:36.428 --> 00:26:37.762
Best decision I ever made.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:40.974
Once you're part of
this Boat Club, you have to be,

00:26:41.057 --> 00:26:43.518
particularly for the six seven
months leading up to the race,

00:26:43.602 --> 00:26:45.562
totally locked in and making
sure that you're getting

00:26:45.604 --> 00:26:47.439
as much out
of yourself as you can.

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:49.441
And we're all expected to do
really well in our studies

00:26:49.482 --> 00:26:51.359
as well, so it
is a bit of a challenge

00:26:51.443 --> 00:26:52.944
and one that's really,
really great learning.

00:26:53.778 --> 00:26:56.156
We usually end the week
on a pretty tough session

00:26:56.239 --> 00:26:58.617
on Sundays, earn the day
off we tend to say.

00:26:58.658 --> 00:27:00.910
Get up a little bit later
than usual on Monday mornings,

00:27:00.952 --> 00:27:04.289
head to Nanna Mex for a Monday
burrito on the student deal.

00:27:05.832 --> 00:27:08.293
You know, when you're sitting
on the start line on race day

00:27:08.335 --> 00:27:10.837
and you know that there's
something like 6 million

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:12.589
people watching on BBC One

00:27:12.631 --> 00:27:14.424
and you know
that there's 200,000 people

00:27:14.466 --> 00:27:16.301
on the banks of the Thames.

00:27:16.384 --> 00:27:18.136
And you're sitting there
and you're absolutely trying

00:27:18.178 --> 00:27:21.306
to do anything but make
a mess of the first stroke

00:27:21.389 --> 00:27:23.350
and end up losing
your crew the race.

00:27:23.433 --> 00:27:25.018
And that's basically all you
can think about, otherwise,

00:27:25.101 --> 00:27:26.978
you do exactly that.

00:27:27.937 --> 00:27:30.065
To hear the roars
of the crowds coming through

00:27:30.148 --> 00:27:31.733
Hammersmith Bridge,
it's a really special

00:27:31.816 --> 00:27:32.859
thing to be a part of.

00:27:33.485 --> 00:27:36.112
So my first plaque
is yet to go up,

00:27:36.154 --> 00:27:38.156
it's somewhere in one
of the corners of the room.

00:27:38.239 --> 00:27:39.574
Oh, there it is.

00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:41.242
Yes, that's my plaque there

00:27:41.326 --> 00:27:43.328
and I'm right in the middle
of the 5-seater.

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:45.705
And I think it's destined
for one of these spaces

00:27:45.789 --> 00:27:47.123
on the walls up here.

00:27:49.167 --> 00:27:51.920
This race has been part
of national culture

00:27:52.003 --> 00:27:53.672
a little bit for so long.

00:27:55.632 --> 00:27:58.259
I thoroughly
enjoy the Oxbridge Boat Race.

00:27:58.551 --> 00:28:01.221
My number two sister went
to Cambridge and Geordie went

00:28:01.304 --> 00:28:04.349
to Oxford and my number five
sister went to Oxford as well.

00:28:04.432 --> 00:28:07.185
So there is
this extraordinary rivalry,

00:28:07.268 --> 00:28:09.354
light blue and dark blue
and obviously as children

00:28:09.437 --> 00:28:11.523
we were always
very excited if somebody sank,

00:28:11.564 --> 00:28:14.401
we were usually
waiting for that.

00:28:15.026 --> 00:28:17.529
IAN MACKELLAN: It was the first
thing I ever saw on television.

00:28:18.071 --> 00:28:20.949
I was walking past a shop front
and there was a crowd

00:28:21.032 --> 00:28:23.660
looking into the window
and I went to join them

00:28:23.702 --> 00:28:27.205
and on a little screen
about that size,

00:28:27.706 --> 00:28:30.041
there was some brown,
shady image.

00:28:32.043 --> 00:28:34.879
And I realised it was moving
image, which was happening

00:28:34.963 --> 00:28:39.175
at that moment and so that was
the first time I saw television.

00:28:40.427 --> 00:28:43.012
Oh, well,
I've always wanted Cambridge

00:28:43.054 --> 00:28:44.556
to win the Boat Race,
because my uncle

00:28:44.597 --> 00:28:45.890
went to Cambridge.

00:28:47.559 --> 00:28:49.310
It's not really a race, is it?

00:28:49.394 --> 00:28:52.230
Because it's the same two
competing for something

00:28:52.272 --> 00:28:54.983
that's largely
irrelevant to everybody else.

00:28:55.567 --> 00:29:00.280
[cheerful keyboard instrumental]

00:29:08.663 --> 00:29:10.540
Cricket originated in England,

00:29:10.582 --> 00:29:13.460
originated in the countryside.
I can't possibly imagine

00:29:13.543 --> 00:29:15.420
what it was like back then,
but look at where

00:29:15.462 --> 00:29:17.130
we've come to now, so.

00:29:18.882 --> 00:29:21.384
Cricket I think is part
of tradition and of course

00:29:21.426 --> 00:29:24.179
Lord's Cricket Ground
is as much about the ceremony

00:29:24.262 --> 00:29:26.014
as I guess the game itself.

00:29:26.097 --> 00:29:29.267
You've got Lord's Cricket
Ground, regarded as the home

00:29:29.309 --> 00:29:31.853
of cricket and it's still
only colonial countries

00:29:31.936 --> 00:29:33.730
who predominantly play cricket,

00:29:33.772 --> 00:29:35.607
which says
everything about the game.

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:37.400
Cricket is essentially,

00:29:37.442 --> 00:29:39.778
a quintessentially
English pastime.

00:29:39.861 --> 00:29:43.281
There are very few games where
you can play for five days,

00:29:43.323 --> 00:29:46.075
go home, do anything
you like in between,

00:29:46.117 --> 00:29:48.161
and at the end of five days,
have a draw

00:29:48.244 --> 00:29:50.872
and everyone be relatively
happy with that result.

00:29:51.331 --> 00:29:54.083
I was brought up
on cricket because at the end

00:29:54.125 --> 00:29:56.461
of our garden
in Wigan was the local

00:29:56.544 --> 00:29:58.421
cricket club and I watched

00:29:58.463 --> 00:30:00.465
their ground
through the seasons.

00:30:01.007 --> 00:30:03.843
In the winter when
it was fallow and snowing,

00:30:03.927 --> 00:30:05.929
and then in the spring
when the grass

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:09.098
could be cut and the players
arrived to practice.

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:11.309
And then at the weekends
when they actually played games,

00:30:11.351 --> 00:30:12.644
I could watch them.

00:30:13.228 --> 00:30:15.814
And I became
not a player of cricket,

00:30:15.855 --> 00:30:17.690
but I was
in the score box marking

00:30:17.774 --> 00:30:20.735
the rather
complicated way in which

00:30:20.819 --> 00:30:22.320
points are scored in cricket.

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:24.489
You go to a cricket match
and you don't have

00:30:24.572 --> 00:30:26.407
to watch it all the time.

00:30:26.491 --> 00:30:28.660
There's plenty of time
to have a chat with the person

00:30:28.701 --> 00:30:31.454
next door to you,
often about cricket.

00:30:32.205 --> 00:30:33.832
"Oh, they're starting again."

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:38.670
To think of it as a game
in which people win or lose,

00:30:38.753 --> 00:30:40.839
no, it's a different
sort of game altogether,

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:42.131
it's the game of life.

00:30:45.885 --> 00:30:48.429
KARL DUNN: Okay, in my house,
there was no cricket,

00:30:48.513 --> 00:30:51.808
there was no rugby,
there was no Aussie Rules,

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:53.434
none of that stuff.
We all sailed.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:56.354
I don't know anything
about team sports.

00:30:56.396 --> 00:30:58.815
Nothing. You're truly
asking the wrong Australian.

00:30:58.857 --> 00:31:00.817
I can't believe they didn't
take away my passport.

00:31:00.859 --> 00:31:02.402
But yes, I don't know
anything about cricket.

00:31:02.861 --> 00:31:03.945
Sorry.

00:31:04.654 --> 00:31:06.447
Her Majesty the Queen
and the Duke of Edinburgh

00:31:06.531 --> 00:31:08.533
many times
attended test matches,

00:31:08.575 --> 00:31:10.702
often meeting the players
on the field of play

00:31:10.785 --> 00:31:12.537
during the tea interval.

00:31:12.579 --> 00:31:14.706
Only on one occasion
has the Queen actually stayed

00:31:14.747 --> 00:31:16.666
for lunch, however.
That was during a test

00:31:16.708 --> 00:31:19.377
match against Australia.
Her favourite tipple,

00:31:19.419 --> 00:31:21.337
Dubonnet, was not present.

00:31:21.379 --> 00:31:24.299
So a member of the kitchen
staff had to be dispatched

00:31:24.382 --> 00:31:26.175
to find an off licence somewhere

00:31:26.217 --> 00:31:28.052
in St John's Wood
or Maida Vale,

00:31:28.094 --> 00:31:29.804
which had a bottle
of Dubonnet in stock.

00:31:29.888 --> 00:31:32.140
And then had to dash back
to the ground in time

00:31:32.223 --> 00:31:35.143
to serve the Queen her favourite
aperitif before lunch.

00:31:35.226 --> 00:31:37.604
Just one of the little,
extra measures that we take

00:31:37.687 --> 00:31:39.147
to ensure our special guests

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:40.732
are well looked after
at this ground.

00:31:48.448 --> 00:31:51.034
EMMA ELWICK-BATES: I lived
in Bloomsbury for several years

00:31:51.075 --> 00:31:54.329
in London and it's an area
that's very special to me.

00:31:54.412 --> 00:31:55.830
And I think what
I love about it,

00:31:55.914 --> 00:31:57.415
other than
the Dickensian heritage,

00:31:57.457 --> 00:32:00.335
is all
the little twists and turns

00:32:00.418 --> 00:32:02.378
and you stumble
upon these small museums

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:04.255
that you may
not have known existed.

00:32:04.297 --> 00:32:07.258
I particularly love
the Sir John Soane's Museum.

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:09.636
And is quite famously
said that the British

00:32:09.719 --> 00:32:11.429
are such avid collectors.

00:32:11.512 --> 00:32:15.516
And there's no better example
of a random, delightful,

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:20.146
at times horrifying, collection
of antiquities hidden away

00:32:20.229 --> 00:32:22.315
on a quiet street in Bloomsbury.

00:32:23.232 --> 00:32:24.776
DR. BRUCE BOUCHER:
People look at these buildings,

00:32:24.817 --> 00:32:27.570
they look at a place
like this and they see

00:32:27.612 --> 00:32:30.657
something that's
really like a fly in amber.

00:32:31.616 --> 00:32:33.868
We're in the library dining room

00:32:33.952 --> 00:32:36.162
of Sir John Soane's townhouse.

00:32:36.245 --> 00:32:38.957
Which became,
after his death, his museum.

00:32:39.290 --> 00:32:42.794
He was appointed
to be the architect of the Bank

00:32:42.835 --> 00:32:45.129
of England and that was probably
his greatest achievement,

00:32:45.171 --> 00:32:50.343
and he was awarded a knighthood.
He was also fortunate

00:32:50.426 --> 00:32:53.513
in marrying an heiress,
Eliza Smith.

00:32:54.806 --> 00:32:57.558
One of the great tragedies
was that only two years

00:32:57.642 --> 00:33:00.937
after they moved into the house,
Eliza Soane died.

00:33:01.729 --> 00:33:07.402
Soane was disconsolate.
The tomb he designed

00:33:07.485 --> 00:33:08.987
a few months
after her death

00:33:09.028 --> 00:33:12.031
and it was
a remarkable departure

00:33:12.115 --> 00:33:13.408
from contemporary tombs.

00:33:14.367 --> 00:33:16.494
Giles Gilbert Scott
must have looked at it

00:33:16.577 --> 00:33:18.246
and being intimate
with the house

00:33:18.329 --> 00:33:19.998
and the collections,
it would have

00:33:20.039 --> 00:33:23.084
come to him as an Edicule,
which offers shelter

00:33:23.167 --> 00:33:24.711
for communication.

00:33:25.003 --> 00:33:27.880
Gilbert Scott,
who designed the phone box,

00:33:27.964 --> 00:33:30.341
he saw this
and thought that shape,

00:33:30.425 --> 00:33:32.802
and I hope he thought
and that love story,

00:33:32.844 --> 00:33:35.138
will be just perfect
for this new invention

00:33:35.179 --> 00:33:36.681
called the phone.

00:33:37.682 --> 00:33:39.976
His inspiration
was a brilliant bit

00:33:40.018 --> 00:33:41.936
of lateral thinking,
if you like.

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:44.313
Gilbert Scott
wanted it to be silver,

00:33:44.355 --> 00:33:48.359
but the Post Office said,
"Oh no, budget, budget"

00:33:48.443 --> 00:33:50.695
and they said it's going
to have to be red.

00:33:50.778 --> 00:33:55.033
And now that's an iconic sense
of Englishness in this country

00:33:55.074 --> 00:33:57.035
and right around the world.

00:33:57.744 --> 00:34:03.374
[soft piano music]

00:34:04.917 --> 00:34:06.544
So we're here today
at Henry Poole.

00:34:06.627 --> 00:34:08.838
At this stage,
we've done now the pattern.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:11.007
We've gone through
the detailing with yourself.

00:34:11.049 --> 00:34:13.384
Back width is good,
shoulder width is good.

00:34:13.468 --> 00:34:16.387
At this point, I'd be asking
you if there's anything you

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:17.889
don't like, anything you want

00:34:17.930 --> 00:34:19.599
to change,
you know, button heights.

00:34:21.059 --> 00:34:23.853
Sleeve length,
they're just a little bit short.

00:34:23.895 --> 00:34:26.105
So I'm just going to put
in a quarter of an inch

00:34:26.189 --> 00:34:28.232
on those and
then you should show about

00:34:28.274 --> 00:34:30.735
a centimetre 3/8 of shirt cuff.

00:34:30.777 --> 00:34:31.861
Sure.

00:34:32.487 --> 00:34:35.698
I mean, I really
like how comfortable it feels

00:34:35.740 --> 00:34:38.076
and how light
and also I like the color.

00:34:38.326 --> 00:34:41.037
Obviously, it's a little bit
livelier than a plain navy,

00:34:41.079 --> 00:34:43.164
but without
being overly flashy, you know,

00:34:43.247 --> 00:34:44.832
if you had a real
electric blue suit,

00:34:44.916 --> 00:34:46.751
then, you know,
how many times are

00:34:46.793 --> 00:34:48.753
you going to wear it
before you get bored

00:34:48.795 --> 00:34:51.255
or people say, "Here he comes in
his electric blue suit again?"

00:34:56.219 --> 00:34:58.846
We have to admire
the entrepreneurial spirit

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:02.725
of the original John Lobb,
who in 1863 saw the Prince

00:35:02.767 --> 00:35:06.604
of Wales in the park and saw
that he left a muddy footprint

00:35:06.687 --> 00:35:09.232
and then he used that
as an imprint to make his first

00:35:09.273 --> 00:35:11.734
riding boots, which he'd
gifted to the Prince of Wales

00:35:11.776 --> 00:35:13.611
before he was King.

00:35:13.653 --> 00:35:16.072
That's how the long
standing relationship

00:35:16.114 --> 00:35:17.782
with this heritage brand,

00:35:17.824 --> 00:35:20.076
the Royal Family and of course,
the Royal Warrant

00:35:20.118 --> 00:35:23.454
that John Lobb has carried
for so long, came about.

00:35:25.456 --> 00:35:26.999
[hammering]

00:35:27.083 --> 00:35:29.043
JONATHAN LOBB:
My great-great-grandfather

00:35:29.127 --> 00:35:30.294
founded the firm.

00:35:30.795 --> 00:35:32.588
The fact that
it's a family run business.

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:34.924
Behind that family run
element of it

00:35:34.966 --> 00:35:37.135
is the fact that
it's an independent business

00:35:37.218 --> 00:35:40.138
and I think it is
that element of independence,

00:35:40.179 --> 00:35:43.266
which is maybe a
quintessentially English trait.

00:35:45.059 --> 00:35:47.770
In World War II,
when my great-uncle had started

00:35:47.812 --> 00:35:50.356
running the firm, there
was a moment when the bombs

00:35:50.439 --> 00:35:52.233
were falling on London.

00:35:52.316 --> 00:35:54.986
One evening he decided
that it would be best to have

00:35:55.027 --> 00:35:57.738
the lasts move out into
the country to keep them safe.

00:35:58.114 --> 00:35:59.991
Early the next day,
the building was bombed

00:36:00.032 --> 00:36:01.325
and it was condemned.

00:36:01.868 --> 00:36:04.704
The lasts are very much
the lifeblood of the firm.

00:36:04.787 --> 00:36:07.582
Keeping those from
being burned was a good move.

00:36:10.334 --> 00:36:12.587
So those are all old names.

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:14.505
Thing is, we've actually
got to the point where lots

00:36:14.589 --> 00:36:16.340
of people don't know
who these people are anymore.

00:36:16.382 --> 00:36:19.260
-Yes.
-David Niven, Lord Olivier.

00:36:19.343 --> 00:36:22.430
This is Peter O'Toole,
Aristotle Onassis.

00:36:23.806 --> 00:36:24.849
Sinatra.

00:36:25.975 --> 00:36:27.852
-FRANK MANNION: Wow.
-JONATHAN: Small feet.

00:36:27.894 --> 00:36:29.854
Those are well used.
We had a lot of work

00:36:29.896 --> 00:36:31.022
for him over the years.

00:36:32.857 --> 00:36:35.735
FRANK MANNION: Jackie Onassis,
she had large feet,

00:36:35.818 --> 00:36:37.778
I have to say.

00:36:38.196 --> 00:36:40.489
Yes, she was quite tall
I think, she was a tall lady.

00:36:40.531 --> 00:36:41.741
FRANK MANNION: Yes.

00:36:43.910 --> 00:36:46.204
Some people,
I have heard, will be asked

00:36:46.287 --> 00:36:48.206
to be buried in their shoes.

00:36:48.247 --> 00:36:50.041
Obviously, their favourite
items that they may have had

00:36:50.082 --> 00:36:51.667
during their lives.

00:36:51.709 --> 00:36:53.920
Well, they do say
you should judge a man by shoes.

00:36:55.588 --> 00:36:57.381
Regardless of what
he might be wearing.

00:36:58.090 --> 00:36:59.800
This is what we're faced with,

00:37:00.259 --> 00:37:03.512
which is this blue suit
which you're having made.

00:37:03.554 --> 00:37:05.264
-FRANK MANNION: Yes.
-By Simon.

00:37:06.057 --> 00:37:08.184
You might prefer something
which is a little bit more,

00:37:08.226 --> 00:37:09.977
sort of
in keeping with the suit.

00:37:11.479 --> 00:37:13.564
The one I think that really
sort of complemented

00:37:13.606 --> 00:37:16.150
what you were wearing
was the double-monk shoe,

00:37:16.234 --> 00:37:18.569
it's designed as quite a sort
of comfortable shoe as well.

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:20.696
Which is good for everyday wear.

00:37:23.783 --> 00:37:28.246
[thoughtful piano instrumental]

00:37:29.372 --> 00:37:32.083
IAN MACKELLAN: British
weather is, for me, perfect.

00:37:33.084 --> 00:37:34.961
It changes every day.

00:37:35.795 --> 00:37:37.004
You never get bored.

00:37:37.713 --> 00:37:41.342
It's reliable
in its unreliability.

00:37:44.178 --> 00:37:46.264
FRED MICHELL: The best cab
service in the world

00:37:46.347 --> 00:37:48.391
is the London taxi driver,
right or wrong?

00:37:48.432 --> 00:37:50.434
SIMON JOHNS: Definitely, Fred.

00:37:50.476 --> 00:37:52.436
Just our origin, it's being
going so long, it's an icon.

00:37:52.478 --> 00:37:55.439
It's like a red bus
or a telephone box.

00:37:55.815 --> 00:37:58.526
Come to London,
you ride in a black cab.

00:37:58.776 --> 00:38:01.070
FRANK MANNION: If I got into
the back of your cab and said,

00:38:01.112 --> 00:38:02.947
"Take me to the umbrella shop."

00:38:02.989 --> 00:38:05.074
DRIVERS: We'd take you
to James Smith &amp; Sons,

00:38:05.116 --> 00:38:07.660
New Oxford St, Bloomsbury.
Of course we know it.

00:38:07.743 --> 00:38:09.495
SIMON: Yes.

00:38:09.578 --> 00:38:11.580
I suppose rain
is quintessentially English,

00:38:11.622 --> 00:38:14.542
hence the umbrella has to also
be quintessentially English

00:38:14.625 --> 00:38:16.210
to protect you
from this ghastly weather.

00:38:16.294 --> 00:38:17.795
Having said that,
today we are blessed

00:38:17.837 --> 00:38:19.630
with some beautiful blue skies.

00:38:20.631 --> 00:38:22.883
OZ CLARKE: I love it
when I see those sort of shops

00:38:22.967 --> 00:38:25.636
which do something
very particular.

00:38:25.678 --> 00:38:29.348
Which are charming,
which say something about us

00:38:29.432 --> 00:38:31.934
and which I would
hate to die out.

00:38:31.976 --> 00:38:35.646
That emporium of umbrellas.

00:38:35.688 --> 00:38:39.942
It's a mad idea
and I'm absolutely delighted

00:38:39.984 --> 00:38:41.193
it's still there.

00:38:41.902 --> 00:38:44.113
PHIL NAISMITH: James Smith
and Sons have been in business

00:38:44.155 --> 00:38:47.783
since 1830 and we're
on to the sixth generation.

00:38:47.825 --> 00:38:49.827
It's quintessential English,

00:38:49.869 --> 00:38:51.871
but it's actually
a little bit more than that.

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:56.000
The umbrella was said
to have started either in Egypt

00:38:56.083 --> 00:38:59.045
or China, and the real
pick up with it came

00:38:59.128 --> 00:39:01.130
with the invention
of steel as part

00:39:01.172 --> 00:39:03.007
of the Industrial Revolution.

00:39:03.049 --> 00:39:05.760
People had more money,
more time to spend on things.

00:39:05.843 --> 00:39:09.221
Slowly then, umbrellas
became popular in London

00:39:09.305 --> 00:39:10.931
as a fashion accessory.

00:39:11.349 --> 00:39:13.517
A good umbrella
becomes part of you.

00:39:13.601 --> 00:39:15.353
It can make you stand up tall,

00:39:15.436 --> 00:39:18.022
but it can also make
you feel good inside as well.

00:39:19.565 --> 00:39:22.360
We've always been
taught you must never open

00:39:22.401 --> 00:39:24.111
your umbrella indoors,
you always have

00:39:24.195 --> 00:39:25.446
to store it wrapped up.

00:39:27.198 --> 00:39:29.825
Part of the folklore, we say,
is where that came from

00:39:29.867 --> 00:39:32.828
is that the salesmen would tell
people that it was bad luck

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:35.623
to leave an umbrella
open indoors.

00:39:35.706 --> 00:39:38.376
So then they'd be
folded up, they'd be wet

00:39:38.417 --> 00:39:40.002
and because
they were made of silk

00:39:40.044 --> 00:39:41.837
in those days,
then they would rot

00:39:41.879 --> 00:39:44.465
and then they'd come
back to buy another umbrella.

00:39:44.924 --> 00:39:47.676
You go to James Smith now,
they've got an enormous

00:39:47.718 --> 00:39:50.638
horseshoe saying,
"Don't worry, it's not bad luck.

00:39:50.721 --> 00:39:52.556
You can do what you
like with your umbrella.

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:55.309
This horseshoe will
protect you." Honestly.

00:39:57.061 --> 00:39:59.647
There's a secret part
in every Englishman which wants

00:39:59.730 --> 00:40:01.524
to be The Gentleman Spy.

00:40:01.565 --> 00:40:03.776
The immaculately
tailored Savile Row suit,

00:40:03.859 --> 00:40:06.070
the rolled up umbrella.
It's as deeply rooted

00:40:06.112 --> 00:40:08.739
in English culture
these days, as cricket is.

00:40:11.242 --> 00:40:14.412
If you've got the money and
if you've got the inclination

00:40:14.453 --> 00:40:18.416
to have someone look after you,
to treat you, to pamper you,

00:40:18.457 --> 00:40:20.501
go to somewhere like Trumper's.

00:40:20.584 --> 00:40:24.505
They may even give you the
cologne that James Bond wore.

00:40:25.589 --> 00:40:28.217
We're at Geo. F. Trumper.

00:40:28.259 --> 00:40:31.971
It's a flagship store,
this has been here 100 years.

00:40:32.054 --> 00:40:34.098
Open the door
in the morning and your body

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:35.850
gets the goose bumps going.

00:40:35.933 --> 00:40:38.185
PAULETTE TRUMPER:
We always give accessories

00:40:38.269 --> 00:40:40.020
to the James Bond films.

00:40:40.104 --> 00:40:42.314
You could miss it if
you didn't know what you were

00:40:42.398 --> 00:40:43.566
looking at in the bathroom,

00:40:43.607 --> 00:40:45.192
but we usually spot them.

00:40:46.193 --> 00:40:47.611
People trust us.

00:40:47.695 --> 00:40:49.238
All the famous
people that come to us

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:51.198
trust that we'll
never mention who they are.

00:40:51.282 --> 00:40:53.451
We never phone magazines
or newspapers when they arrive.

00:40:53.534 --> 00:40:55.536
They come in,
feel very relaxed,

00:40:55.619 --> 00:40:58.122
as if nobody's recognized them.

00:40:58.205 --> 00:40:59.498
FRANK MANNION: Ian McKellen?

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:01.125
Yes.

00:41:02.168 --> 00:41:03.836
Oh, I have been there once.

00:41:04.795 --> 00:41:06.172
Yes, to get a shave.

00:41:07.131 --> 00:41:08.382
Yes, it was very good.

00:41:09.008 --> 00:41:10.885
PAULETTE TRUMPER: So even
the current Prime Minister

00:41:10.968 --> 00:41:13.929
was a customer of
our other branch in St James'.

00:41:13.971 --> 00:41:15.764
We actually
don't do his hair anymore,

00:41:15.806 --> 00:41:18.058
which is probably
noticeable by the haircuts.

00:41:19.101 --> 00:41:20.895
What, he pays
to get that haircut?

00:41:20.978 --> 00:41:22.396
I just assumed his mum did it.

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:23.814
That's ridiculous.

00:41:26.150 --> 00:41:29.320
[birds tweeting]

00:41:32.823 --> 00:41:36.494
I love looking at gardens,
sitting in gardens,

00:41:36.535 --> 00:41:37.912
walking through gardens.

00:41:38.621 --> 00:41:41.165
I am hopeless
at being a gardener.

00:41:41.999 --> 00:41:43.834
JAMIE HARRIS: Sir Edmund Loder
that classic example

00:41:43.876 --> 00:41:45.503
of an English eccentric,

00:41:45.544 --> 00:41:47.046
having that sort
of passion that drive

00:41:47.129 --> 00:41:49.423
to build a garden
on such a grand scale,

00:41:49.507 --> 00:41:52.051
a great deal of it
by his own hand.

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:55.137
Leonardslee's
often referred to as one

00:41:55.179 --> 00:41:57.515
of the most important
woodland gardens in England.

00:41:58.057 --> 00:42:00.100
A lot of the English
garden design was taking

00:42:00.184 --> 00:42:01.977
influence from Europe.

00:42:02.019 --> 00:42:03.896
After the English landscape
movement and the woodland

00:42:03.979 --> 00:42:05.981
garden explosion,
suddenly English gardens

00:42:06.023 --> 00:42:07.233
were influencing Europe.

00:42:07.858 --> 00:42:09.610
And apparently Hitler
had his eye on Leonardslee

00:42:09.693 --> 00:42:11.612
as his new home.

00:42:11.695 --> 00:42:13.781
The great English film
&lt;i&gt;Black Narcissus&lt;/i&gt; was shot here.

00:42:13.864 --> 00:42:16.825
Sir Edmund created
a landscape which could pass

00:42:16.867 --> 00:42:18.327
for the Himalayas, certainly

00:42:18.369 --> 00:42:20.037
with the rhododendrons and
azaleas.

00:42:20.079 --> 00:42:22.039
It's a place to get lost in,
a place to wander

00:42:22.122 --> 00:42:23.791
and every day
it's a privilege and an honor

00:42:23.874 --> 00:42:25.876
to come in and work
in this classic example

00:42:25.918 --> 00:42:27.169
of an English woodland garden.

00:42:29.380 --> 00:42:30.965
PROFESSOR MICHAEL WHEELER:
One of the definitions

00:42:31.048 --> 00:42:33.300
of Britishness
is a love of gardens.

00:42:33.384 --> 00:42:36.762
So even if you're living on
the fifth floor of a high rise

00:42:36.845 --> 00:42:38.472
and if you have
a little balcony outside,

00:42:38.556 --> 00:42:40.683
it's full of pots
with things growing in it.

00:42:40.724 --> 00:42:42.184
It's extraordinary.

00:42:42.226 --> 00:42:43.978
The British love gardening.

00:42:44.061 --> 00:42:46.647
The English are obsessed
with gardens and that probably

00:42:46.730 --> 00:42:49.483
culminates with the Chelsea
Flower Show every year.

00:42:49.567 --> 00:42:52.570
NICOLA HALE: It brings
a real mix of different types

00:42:52.653 --> 00:42:54.989
of design
in one location for people

00:42:55.072 --> 00:42:56.991
to just get inspiration
from for their own gardens

00:42:57.074 --> 00:42:59.159
and for
their own houses even as well.

00:42:59.243 --> 00:43:01.745
This is essentially like,
I suppose, the Champions League

00:43:01.787 --> 00:43:04.039
of gardening that
I'm visiting today, I think,

00:43:04.081 --> 00:43:05.833
and having
had a little walk around,

00:43:05.916 --> 00:43:07.876
I'm definitely
getting that sense.

00:43:07.918 --> 00:43:10.337
This is fantastic, I mean it's
if you like, in our back garden.

00:43:11.797 --> 00:43:14.341
FRANK MANNION: Chelsea is the
quintessential British occasion.

00:43:14.425 --> 00:43:16.176
It is. One of them.

00:43:16.635 --> 00:43:18.095
FRANK MANNION: What is
so quintessentially British

00:43:18.137 --> 00:43:19.972
about the Chelsea Flower Show?

00:43:20.055 --> 00:43:21.599
That we're here
in this weather with no coats

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:23.434
and with nothing else on.

00:43:23.517 --> 00:43:25.060
SOF MCVEIGH:
And it's fun, and it's fun.

00:43:25.102 --> 00:43:27.021
You get the chance
to put a pretty frock on

00:43:27.104 --> 00:43:29.273
and you see beautiful things
and chat to lovely people.

00:43:29.356 --> 00:43:31.609
And we are a Royal Borough,
because Queen Victoria,

00:43:31.692 --> 00:43:33.736
on her deathbed,
decreed that the borough

00:43:33.777 --> 00:43:35.863
she was born in should
become a Royal Borough.

00:43:35.946 --> 00:43:38.157
So that's why we're the Royal
Borough of Kensington &amp; Chelsea.

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:39.658
And later on this afternoon,
we'll be welcoming

00:43:39.742 --> 00:43:41.368
some of the Royal Family,

00:43:41.452 --> 00:43:43.245
some of the people
that live in our boroughs.

00:43:43.287 --> 00:43:46.290
So it all comes together
on our RHS Chelsea Flower Show.

00:43:46.332 --> 00:43:53.213
♪♪♪

00:43:59.136 --> 00:44:02.431
It's truly brilliant
just to meet a fellow queen.

00:44:02.473 --> 00:44:06.060
Especially today, where
it is the art form of gardening

00:44:06.143 --> 00:44:07.561
at the Chelsea Flower Show.

00:44:07.645 --> 00:44:09.271
And the art form of drag.

00:44:09.313 --> 00:44:13.275
And just a gorgeous
British icon, as well.

00:44:13.317 --> 00:44:14.860
WOMAN: Absolutely.
Here here.

00:44:14.943 --> 00:44:17.154
What a pleasure
it is to meet Judi Dench.

00:44:18.364 --> 00:44:20.032
Thank you.

00:44:20.115 --> 00:44:22.076
Is it really bad as well
that I really just wanted

00:44:22.159 --> 00:44:23.786
to joke and be like,
"It's such a pleasure

00:44:23.827 --> 00:44:25.120
to meet Maggie Smith."

00:44:26.038 --> 00:44:28.916
But no, it truly is a pleasure
to meet you, honestly.

00:44:28.999 --> 00:44:30.834
And you, you look beautiful.

00:44:30.876 --> 00:44:32.044
Thank you.

00:44:35.547 --> 00:44:38.467
I was making handbags
in the early seventies

00:44:38.509 --> 00:44:42.221
and the opportunity
came to buy Launer.

00:44:42.304 --> 00:44:45.099
When I bought the company,
it had the Royal Warrant,

00:44:45.182 --> 00:44:47.184
but it'd not been really used.

00:44:47.893 --> 00:44:50.229
I obviously wanted
to go direct to the palace

00:44:50.312 --> 00:44:52.064
and slowly managed to do that.

00:44:52.690 --> 00:44:54.983
Oh, the Queen
is a delightful person.

00:44:55.234 --> 00:44:57.194
Just like the lady next door.

00:44:57.236 --> 00:45:00.322
Very interested in what we do,
because when she was

00:45:00.364 --> 00:45:03.325
in the factory, she spent
a good afternoon with us.

00:45:04.368 --> 00:45:07.788
EMMA ELWICK-BATES: It's reported
that the Queen has 200 handbags,

00:45:07.871 --> 00:45:10.749
predominantly
Launer London handbags.

00:45:10.833 --> 00:45:14.044
If I was lucky enough
to be invited for an audience

00:45:14.086 --> 00:45:16.755
with the Queen, I think
the most mortifying thing for me

00:45:16.839 --> 00:45:18.674
would be if she moved
her Launer handbag

00:45:18.716 --> 00:45:21.510
from her left to her right arm.

00:45:21.552 --> 00:45:24.513
It is suggested that's her
indication to the courtiers

00:45:24.555 --> 00:45:25.723
to come and rescue her.

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:29.518
The Launer handbag, as well
as being a stylish accessory,

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:33.689
has become her escape route
in difficult social situations.

00:45:33.731 --> 00:45:35.858
It's an understated
British way of getting out

00:45:35.899 --> 00:45:38.235
of an awkward situation.

00:45:39.486 --> 00:45:41.530
We're very lucky
to be in&lt;i&gt; The Crown.&lt;/i&gt;

00:45:41.572 --> 00:45:44.700
The costume designers
were interested in coming

00:45:44.742 --> 00:45:47.745
to the showroom, because
they knew that Mrs. Thatcher

00:45:47.786 --> 00:45:50.789
and Her Majesty
carried my handbags.

00:45:50.873 --> 00:45:54.376
That's very intriguing
that two silhouetted,

00:45:54.418 --> 00:45:57.838
famous, pair of ladies
chose the same handbag brand.

00:45:58.338 --> 00:46:01.175
This was the Traviata,
which is the Queen's.

00:46:01.759 --> 00:46:03.510
I know this
is a beautiful white one.

00:46:03.594 --> 00:46:07.264
Mrs. Thatcher had the Olympia,
which was the nearest

00:46:07.306 --> 00:46:10.768
to the bag that she carried
when she bought from me.

00:46:11.185 --> 00:46:12.436
Both great ladies.

00:46:13.437 --> 00:46:16.940
[string instrumental]

00:46:16.982 --> 00:46:18.776
LOUIS BARNES: James Purdey
&amp; Sons have been making guns

00:46:18.817 --> 00:46:20.569
for quite a long time now.

00:46:20.611 --> 00:46:23.113
They are probably the finest
gunmaker in the world,

00:46:23.155 --> 00:46:24.948
as British gunmakers are.

00:46:24.990 --> 00:46:27.326
These take around about
two and a half, three years

00:46:27.409 --> 00:46:29.453
to build, so
this is a real work of art.

00:46:29.536 --> 00:46:32.039
New this would be 100,000 plus.

00:46:32.831 --> 00:46:34.291
You will see
someone using one of these,

00:46:34.333 --> 00:46:35.959
particularly
on a day like today,

00:46:36.001 --> 00:46:37.711
which is the
Glorious Twelfth.

00:46:37.795 --> 00:46:39.713
It's probably the most
exhilarating sport of all,

00:46:39.797 --> 00:46:41.548
bird shooting, I think.

00:46:41.632 --> 00:46:43.967
They can beat on the wing
at up to 70 miles an hour,

00:46:44.009 --> 00:46:45.511
which is a real sport.

00:46:45.594 --> 00:46:48.555
Hitting them is quite a feat.

00:46:49.014 --> 00:46:51.600
The Royals are traditionally
very involved in country sports,

00:46:51.642 --> 00:46:53.268
particularly shooting.

00:46:53.310 --> 00:46:55.687
They're quite well known
to be part of that scene.

00:46:55.771 --> 00:46:58.065
That quintessential
British scene, yes.

00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:01.944
IAN MACKELLAN: I think
I was brought up to think

00:47:01.985 --> 00:47:03.362
it was cruel to kill animals.

00:47:04.071 --> 00:47:06.740
And the idea
of the posh folks going off

00:47:06.824 --> 00:47:08.450
and the centre
of their activity,

00:47:08.492 --> 00:47:10.953
being killing something.

00:47:11.370 --> 00:47:13.914
I think you've got
to be of a certain frame

00:47:13.997 --> 00:47:15.624
of mind of which I am not.

00:47:16.208 --> 00:47:18.293
I'd much rather
they were gathered together,

00:47:18.335 --> 00:47:20.796
the central
point of the weekend

00:47:20.838 --> 00:47:24.091
was when they all dressed up and
did charades, played charades,

00:47:24.174 --> 00:47:25.717
put on a little pantomime.

00:47:27.177 --> 00:47:28.345
Shooting a bird...

00:47:28.387 --> 00:47:29.555
Aww.

00:47:29.638 --> 00:47:31.515
If that's essentially English,

00:47:31.557 --> 00:47:35.269
it's not an Englishness
that I can respond to at all.

00:47:35.352 --> 00:47:38.105
I look at the Glorious Twelfth
and think it is a load

00:47:38.188 --> 00:47:40.816
of baloney
as far as I'm concerned.

00:47:40.858 --> 00:47:42.484
I'm quite grateful
that they're not

00:47:42.526 --> 00:47:44.361
blapping away
at the grouse all year long,

00:47:44.403 --> 00:47:48.991
but it's a quite absurd
piece of gastronomic elitism.

00:47:49.032 --> 00:47:53.036
I don't know anybody who eats
grouse on August the 12th,

00:47:53.078 --> 00:47:55.789
but I'm sure if we went to some
of the smart hotels in London,

00:47:55.873 --> 00:47:59.293
they would serve it to me for
an astronomical amount of money,

00:47:59.376 --> 00:48:01.211
which I do not wish to pay.

00:48:01.920 --> 00:48:04.381
We're at the Stafford Hotel,
and today we're

00:48:04.464 --> 00:48:07.885
in the Game Bird, which is our
wonderful signature restaurant.

00:48:08.385 --> 00:48:10.804
This hotel
has been here since 1912,

00:48:10.888 --> 00:48:13.765
but Glorious Twelfth
goes back to 1853.

00:48:14.141 --> 00:48:16.852
So ever since that day,
we've been serving grouse

00:48:16.894 --> 00:48:19.521
on the 12th of August
and get excited by it.

00:48:19.563 --> 00:48:21.773
It was shot this morning
and is on its way

00:48:21.857 --> 00:48:24.067
from the North Yorkshire Moors.

00:48:26.695 --> 00:48:29.072
It's the Glorious Twelfth,
first grouse of the season.

00:48:29.114 --> 00:48:30.574
We have it here at The Stafford.

00:48:31.533 --> 00:48:32.993
STUART PROCTER:
It's a very discreet place.

00:48:33.076 --> 00:48:34.661
So we're not about celebrity,

00:48:34.745 --> 00:48:37.247
we're not about royalty,
but they all come here.

00:48:37.331 --> 00:48:39.499
Her Majesty,
the Queen visits the Stafford.

00:48:39.750 --> 00:48:42.169
The Queen Mother used to visit
for afternoon tea weekly

00:48:42.252 --> 00:48:43.921
walking up from Clarence House.

00:48:44.004 --> 00:48:46.423
The cellars served the King
of England when he lived

00:48:46.506 --> 00:48:47.758
in St. James's Palace.

00:48:47.799 --> 00:48:49.676
We served the port to the king.

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:51.595
It is a very special place.

00:48:55.098 --> 00:48:56.600
SIMON CUNDEY: We're going
to be getting through

00:48:56.642 --> 00:48:58.268
what's called the blessing.

00:48:58.310 --> 00:49:00.145
The blessing involves
the final fitting.

00:49:00.228 --> 00:49:02.105
We're looking at the small
details now and the general

00:49:02.147 --> 00:49:05.108
comfort that we hear from you
and how you enjoy the suit.

00:49:05.150 --> 00:49:08.946
Also, we have the extra cloth
there, which I must say Frank,

00:49:08.987 --> 00:49:10.906
if any prosperity takes place,

00:49:10.948 --> 00:49:13.450
we can have four inches
of extra cloth inside to allow

00:49:13.533 --> 00:49:14.952
that difference in inches.

00:49:15.035 --> 00:49:17.621
So do not haste,
we are there for you.

00:49:19.039 --> 00:49:20.332
This is good to go now.

00:49:20.916 --> 00:49:24.628
[string instrumental]

00:49:24.670 --> 00:49:26.505
PROFESSOR MICHAEL WHEELER:
Of course, in reality,

00:49:26.588 --> 00:49:28.298
I couldn't show you around
because it's members only.

00:49:28.340 --> 00:49:30.008
FRANK MANNION: Absolutely.

00:49:30.342 --> 00:49:31.802
We're rule breakers.

00:49:33.178 --> 00:49:36.223
So let me just
show you some things here

00:49:36.306 --> 00:49:40.060
in the South Library
that are quite interesting.

00:49:40.978 --> 00:49:43.814
This is Faraday's wheelchair.

00:49:43.897 --> 00:49:49.945
Faraday, our great scientist,
was also the honorary secretary

00:49:49.987 --> 00:49:53.699
just at the very beginning
of the club's history.

00:49:54.533 --> 00:49:57.160
And there
we have Dickens' chair.

00:49:57.953 --> 00:50:00.288
And this incredibly
tall arrangement,

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:03.250
with the three tiers,
was the cheapest way

00:50:03.333 --> 00:50:07.129
of accommodating this huge
number of books in one room.

00:50:07.713 --> 00:50:11.133
The only problem is that a lot
of older members who have

00:50:11.174 --> 00:50:14.094
vertigo are terrified
of going up there,

00:50:14.177 --> 00:50:16.888
so we send the library staff up
to find the books.

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:18.473
I can well imagine that.

00:50:18.515 --> 00:50:19.808
[chuckling]

00:50:20.350 --> 00:50:21.601
PROFESSOR MICHEAL WHEELER:
The Athenaeum does have

00:50:21.685 --> 00:50:23.061
an amazing role of honor.

00:50:23.353 --> 00:50:27.232
Charles Dickens,
Charles Darwin, Churchill,

00:50:27.315 --> 00:50:30.694
J.M.W. Turner, who does
those wonderful interiors,

00:50:31.028 --> 00:50:32.279
member of the club.

00:50:34.031 --> 00:50:35.449
Wherever you look in a way.

00:50:36.575 --> 00:50:38.493
BRIAN WALDY: The clubs
went through a very hard time

00:50:38.535 --> 00:50:40.495
in Pall Mall and in St James'.

00:50:40.537 --> 00:50:42.205
And they were out of favor,

00:50:42.289 --> 00:50:45.000
they were old fashioned,
they were seen to be a relic

00:50:45.042 --> 00:50:46.668
of the 19th century.

00:50:46.710 --> 00:50:49.046
If you read the novels
of Dickens and Thackeray,

00:50:49.087 --> 00:50:51.506
there always seemed to be
somebody going off to the club

00:50:51.548 --> 00:50:54.009
and somebody coming
back from Parliament to report

00:50:54.051 --> 00:50:56.053
over a nice glass of Port.

00:50:56.553 --> 00:50:58.680
NEVILLE JAMES: I've been a
member of a private members club

00:50:58.722 --> 00:51:00.182
purely and simply because

00:51:00.223 --> 00:51:01.808
I've got it as
part of a promotion

00:51:01.892 --> 00:51:03.769
and part of my work
and what I was doing.

00:51:03.852 --> 00:51:07.064
But average person, private
member's club, is irrelevant.

00:51:07.105 --> 00:51:08.523
That's out of our league.

00:51:08.565 --> 00:51:10.317
-Yeah.
-That's out of our league.

00:51:10.400 --> 00:51:13.028
We are just London boys.

00:51:13.070 --> 00:51:15.322
That's another...

00:51:15.405 --> 00:51:16.823
It's another world for us.

00:51:16.907 --> 00:51:18.033
Another world, exactly, yeah.

00:51:18.075 --> 00:51:19.409
But they're lovely places.

00:51:19.493 --> 00:51:21.244
-Apparently.
-Yeah.

00:51:21.286 --> 00:51:22.913
We only go to the main door,

00:51:22.996 --> 00:51:24.581
we don't get invited in,
we just drop off.

00:51:26.083 --> 00:51:29.878
It's a very
useful way of making sure

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:32.255
that you can meet someone
and entertain them

00:51:32.297 --> 00:51:33.965
in the middle of London,

00:51:34.049 --> 00:51:37.928
but I would say it's more
a functional thing for me

00:51:37.969 --> 00:51:41.389
than part
of my being and identity.

00:51:41.431 --> 00:51:43.934
IAN MCKELLEN: The only
real advantage of a club

00:51:43.975 --> 00:51:46.103
would be that it'd be a place

00:51:46.186 --> 00:51:48.230
where you could
go and sit quietly.

00:51:48.271 --> 00:51:50.607
But who might come up to you

00:51:50.649 --> 00:51:53.610
and want to
have a good long chat?

00:51:53.693 --> 00:51:56.196
Actors have a number of clubs

00:51:56.279 --> 00:51:58.031
that they could
be expected to join.

00:51:58.115 --> 00:52:00.659
The Garrick, but women
aren't allowed to be members

00:52:00.742 --> 00:52:01.993
of the Garrick Club.

00:52:02.077 --> 00:52:03.912
Why would I want to go to a club

00:52:03.954 --> 00:52:05.789
that wouldn't have women in it?
I don't know.

00:52:06.748 --> 00:52:08.583
The men's clubs,
they can go there,

00:52:08.625 --> 00:52:10.961
they can sit,
they can talk about racing

00:52:11.002 --> 00:52:12.546
and Backgammon and things

00:52:12.629 --> 00:52:14.756
and I don't
necessarily want to do that.

00:52:14.798 --> 00:52:16.466
You also know where they are.

00:52:16.508 --> 00:52:17.968
That's probably quite positive.

00:52:18.009 --> 00:52:19.970
You shouldn't
regard them as exclusive,

00:52:20.053 --> 00:52:22.264
just an opportunity
to meet other people

00:52:22.305 --> 00:52:24.933
and to widen your
own circle of friends

00:52:24.975 --> 00:52:28.436
and the bonhomie and the banter
that can arise from it.

00:52:28.478 --> 00:52:29.938
It's fun.

00:52:29.980 --> 00:52:31.565
It's quite British.

00:52:33.441 --> 00:52:35.360
PROFESSOR MICHAEL WHEELER:
John Wilson Croker,

00:52:35.443 --> 00:52:39.072
he saw that in London, there
were lots of political clubs,

00:52:39.156 --> 00:52:41.867
there were lots of what
were known as fashionable clubs,

00:52:41.950 --> 00:52:43.994
really, for the aristocracy,
if you like.

00:52:44.035 --> 00:52:47.664
But what was needed
perhaps was a club for writers

00:52:47.747 --> 00:52:50.125
and artists and scientists,

00:52:50.167 --> 00:52:52.919
which would not
be about politics

00:52:53.003 --> 00:52:55.630
and would not be about class.

00:52:55.672 --> 00:52:59.593
The club built
this Grecian temple

00:52:59.676 --> 00:53:01.928
and under the auspices of Athena

00:53:02.012 --> 00:53:03.847
and that sense
of classical wisdom,

00:53:03.889 --> 00:53:05.724
the scientists, artists,

00:53:05.807 --> 00:53:08.768
writers, judges,
cabinet ministers,

00:53:08.852 --> 00:53:13.231
bishops, whatever their views,
they could get along together.

00:53:13.315 --> 00:53:17.777
A place at
the Athenaeum was like gold.

00:53:17.861 --> 00:53:21.114
This is a very
British institution,

00:53:21.198 --> 00:53:22.782
which suits me very well.

00:53:22.866 --> 00:53:24.993
It reflects aspects of Britain

00:53:25.035 --> 00:53:27.537
and its history that I warm to.

00:53:27.579 --> 00:53:31.666
You know, inclusiveness,
liberalism and conservatism.

00:53:31.708 --> 00:53:34.419
And one of the things you have
to be very careful about here,

00:53:34.502 --> 00:53:37.255
at club table when you
don't know whose opposite you,

00:53:37.339 --> 00:53:40.217
is you might be
sounding off about some subject

00:53:40.258 --> 00:53:42.427
and you find
the person opposite you,

00:53:42.510 --> 00:53:45.055
is the world's
leading expert in it.

00:53:45.096 --> 00:53:49.684
So you tend to find people
around here are quite cautious

00:53:49.726 --> 00:53:53.146
to check on who's present.

00:53:53.230 --> 00:53:54.898
That's happened to me.

00:53:54.981 --> 00:53:57.234
Terrifying.

00:53:57.317 --> 00:54:00.362
In terms
of the membership of this club,

00:54:00.403 --> 00:54:03.406
what I've tracked
is the story of Britain,

00:54:03.448 --> 00:54:06.952
because Britain has
become much more dominated

00:54:07.035 --> 00:54:10.705
by the middle class
than by the upper class.

00:54:10.747 --> 00:54:12.958
You're aware of classes.

00:54:13.041 --> 00:54:16.753
You know Shaw said, "When an
Englishman opens his mouth..."

00:54:16.836 --> 00:54:19.047
[laughs]
"...people judge him."

00:54:19.089 --> 00:54:22.008
The Americans are
quite good at class, actually.

00:54:22.092 --> 00:54:24.052
Particularly among
the upper echelons.

00:54:24.094 --> 00:54:26.388
But the British have
been very class-conscious

00:54:26.429 --> 00:54:28.014
and some still are.

00:54:28.098 --> 00:54:30.600
But I think there's
been a big change there.

00:54:30.684 --> 00:54:33.144
I think there's
been a big change there.

00:54:33.228 --> 00:54:37.023
Has the class system
changed so sufficiently

00:54:37.107 --> 00:54:39.150
that we're able to move forward?

00:54:39.234 --> 00:54:44.197
It probably has, because
probably there's more people

00:54:44.281 --> 00:54:47.450
from various backgrounds
making vast amounts of money.

00:54:47.534 --> 00:54:49.202
I suppose that's
the new class system

00:54:49.286 --> 00:54:50.787
now in our country, isn't it?

00:54:50.870 --> 00:54:53.206
People who have money.
People don't have money.

00:55:02.132 --> 00:55:03.466
Neil.

00:55:04.467 --> 00:55:06.136
How are you?

00:55:06.177 --> 00:55:07.595
NEIL MCLAREN:
Frank, nice to see you again.

00:55:07.637 --> 00:55:09.389
Nice to see you too, absolutely.

00:55:09.472 --> 00:55:11.099
Okay Frank, so here we are,

00:55:11.141 --> 00:55:12.559
all ready for
your second fitting.

00:55:12.642 --> 00:55:14.269
You'll see a slight difference

00:55:14.311 --> 00:55:15.895
to when you were
last here in so much

00:55:15.979 --> 00:55:17.647
as we've now made
the bespoke shoe trees.

00:55:17.689 --> 00:55:20.984
Which my younger brother
made in our tree making section.

00:55:21.026 --> 00:55:22.819
He's a fairly new--
new character.

00:55:22.902 --> 00:55:25.113
He's only been here
30 years or so, so yes,

00:55:25.155 --> 00:55:26.740
we're still trying to train him.

00:55:26.823 --> 00:55:28.616
NEIL MCLAREN:
He's a lifer, he's a lifer.

00:55:28.658 --> 00:55:31.244
For sure, but most of us are,
most of us are so, yes.

00:55:31.328 --> 00:55:33.663
So the trees are also bespoke

00:55:33.747 --> 00:55:36.166
and we're left with this
beautifully varnished shoe tree,

00:55:36.207 --> 00:55:39.127
which is an exact
replica of your foot also.

00:55:39.169 --> 00:55:41.880
So, it's got the correct width
and contours that your foot has,

00:55:41.963 --> 00:55:43.673
so the shoe will
never shrink back,

00:55:43.715 --> 00:55:46.009
you'd always
maintain that size and shape,

00:55:46.051 --> 00:55:49.137
which is very important
for the longevity of the shoe.

00:55:49.179 --> 00:55:51.264
JONATHAN LOBB:
The double monk is a shoe

00:55:51.348 --> 00:55:53.141
that's been part
of the firm's history

00:55:53.183 --> 00:55:55.518
since it was
created by my grandfather.

00:55:55.602 --> 00:55:57.854
It has a sort
of timeless quality to it.

00:55:57.937 --> 00:56:01.274
NEIL MCLAREN: So you've got
the oak bark sole hammered

00:56:01.358 --> 00:56:04.486
and shaped to your last
and hand-stitched onto the weld.

00:56:04.527 --> 00:56:06.488
The heel was built up with--

00:56:06.529 --> 00:56:09.240
There's no block heel, this
is individual layers of leather.

00:56:09.324 --> 00:56:11.368
Every pair of shoes goes through

00:56:11.409 --> 00:56:13.161
four different
craftsman's hands.

00:56:13.203 --> 00:56:14.954
We've got the closer who
makes the beautiful uppers,

00:56:15.038 --> 00:56:16.498
the shoemaker who's
stitched all the sole

00:56:16.539 --> 00:56:18.249
and the heel together.

00:56:18.333 --> 00:56:20.126
And then the tree makers
who make the wooden inserts

00:56:20.210 --> 00:56:22.003
to go in afterwards.

00:56:22.045 --> 00:56:23.630
So all these guys are
highly trained, highly skilled.

00:56:23.713 --> 00:56:25.340
It's very much
a team collective effort.

00:56:25.382 --> 00:56:27.050
I get to see you
and get all praise

00:56:27.092 --> 00:56:28.510
what a beautiful shoe it is,

00:56:28.551 --> 00:56:30.136
so I get
the glory when they fit well,

00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:31.554
but by the same token,

00:56:31.596 --> 00:56:33.056
I also get
the grief if they don't.

00:56:33.139 --> 00:56:34.724
So I guess it's
a double-edged sword.

00:56:37.018 --> 00:56:38.770
FRANK MANNION:
I kind of feel like

00:56:38.853 --> 00:56:40.522
I'd want to sleep
in them if I could.

00:56:40.563 --> 00:56:42.565
[laughing]

00:56:42.607 --> 00:56:46.861
If you're invited to one of our
many amazing country houses

00:56:46.903 --> 00:56:49.364
for the weekend,
you wouldn't go wrong

00:56:49.406 --> 00:56:51.032
by wearing a Henry Poole suit

00:56:51.074 --> 00:56:53.243
and a John Lobb
shoe for your arrival.

00:56:57.622 --> 00:57:00.917
I'm sure you
recognize this room.

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:04.754
Oh wow. I do, the library.

00:57:04.796 --> 00:57:07.465
Well, there are
many portraits in it.

00:57:07.549 --> 00:57:10.260
There's one there
of the 1st Earl of Carnarvon.

00:57:10.301 --> 00:57:12.762
Obviously, Geordie
and I have got to number eight.

00:57:12.804 --> 00:57:15.473
What's quite special
about Highclere,

00:57:15.557 --> 00:57:18.643
is there has been
an estate here, a home here,

00:57:18.726 --> 00:57:20.937
if you like,
for at least 1200 years.

00:57:20.979 --> 00:57:24.107
So one of the most special
things about this building,

00:57:24.149 --> 00:57:27.277
heritage, gardens,
home is the longevity.

00:57:27.318 --> 00:57:30.405
I think the idea that
you have this great perspective

00:57:30.447 --> 00:57:33.241
looking backwards,
a sense of an anchor,

00:57:33.283 --> 00:57:36.953
perhaps a sense of reassurance
in quite a changeable world.

00:57:37.036 --> 00:57:38.913
We've been here a while.

00:57:38.955 --> 00:57:41.791
In 1922, Lord Carnarvon
and Highclere Castle

00:57:41.833 --> 00:57:44.419
were front page news
around the world.

00:57:44.461 --> 00:57:48.214
Against all the odds, Lord
Carnarvon and Howard Carter,

00:57:48.298 --> 00:57:51.384
two maverick Englishmen,
discovered the steps,

00:57:51.468 --> 00:57:54.804
the 22 steps which led
down to the Tomb of Tutankhamun.

00:57:54.846 --> 00:57:56.973
That glint of gold.

00:57:57.015 --> 00:58:01.561
That glimpse back into a
Pharaonic world 3500 years old,

00:58:01.644 --> 00:58:05.148
captured the whole
world's imagination.

00:58:05.231 --> 00:58:09.235
What a story to have
at Highclere Castle.

00:58:10.612 --> 00:58:12.655
My father-in-law had
always told me the story,

00:58:12.697 --> 00:58:15.575
the end of World War II,
he and some of his Army friends

00:58:15.658 --> 00:58:18.870
with Princess Elizabeth
and Princess Margaret ran around

00:58:18.953 --> 00:58:21.039
the streets
of London and did the conga

00:58:21.122 --> 00:58:24.292
and just celebrated
the end of World War II.

00:58:24.334 --> 00:58:27.128
And they were all
standing outside the black gates

00:58:27.170 --> 00:58:28.671
of Buckingham Palace.

00:58:28.755 --> 00:58:31.841
My father-in-law
with Princess Elizabeth

00:58:31.925 --> 00:58:33.843
and Margaret shouting
up to the King and Queen,

00:58:33.885 --> 00:58:35.845
"Long live the King and Queen."

00:58:35.887 --> 00:58:38.264
They'd always remained friends

00:58:38.348 --> 00:58:39.682
and she had a love of racing,

00:58:39.766 --> 00:58:41.392
so he was her racing manager,

00:58:41.476 --> 00:58:44.521
but her racing friend above all.

00:58:45.730 --> 00:58:48.191
Geordie, my husband,
and I took over the Castle

00:58:48.233 --> 00:58:50.652
after his father had sadly died.

00:58:50.693 --> 00:58:53.780
His parents had
never lived in it.

00:58:53.863 --> 00:58:56.115
It reminds you that a home

00:58:56.199 --> 00:58:58.785
is about welcoming
friends to the door.

00:58:58.868 --> 00:59:00.995
Julian and Emma Fellowes
were good friends

00:59:01.037 --> 00:59:02.830
and I thought it'd be fun

00:59:02.872 --> 00:59:05.041
and I love putting
together weekend house parties,

00:59:05.083 --> 00:59:08.545
it was where this house started
in Victorian times.

00:59:08.586 --> 00:59:12.090
So Julian Fellowes
said that he wrote Downton

00:59:12.173 --> 00:59:14.133
with Highclere in mind.

00:59:14.217 --> 00:59:16.636
None of us dreamt or
guessed how successful

00:59:16.719 --> 00:59:18.513
&lt;i&gt;Downton Abbey&lt;/i&gt; would be,

00:59:18.555 --> 00:59:20.890
how we'd all fall in love
with the different characters,

00:59:20.932 --> 00:59:23.226
whether it's Bates
or Anne or Lady Mary.

00:59:23.268 --> 00:59:26.229
It's really Julian Fellowes'
excellent writing

00:59:26.271 --> 00:59:30.692
and I guess the way Maggie Smith
delivers all her lines

00:59:30.733 --> 00:59:33.528
so perfectly.

00:59:34.904 --> 00:59:36.698
It's just making people happy.

00:59:36.739 --> 00:59:38.908
That's all Highclere
wants to do.

00:59:38.992 --> 00:59:41.160
Hats have almost disappeared.

00:59:41.244 --> 00:59:43.079
Ties have almost disappeared.

00:59:43.121 --> 00:59:45.290
Well, we'll be
naked next I should think.

00:59:46.416 --> 00:59:50.378
Lock's dates back to 1676.

00:59:50.420 --> 00:59:52.380
We're the oldest hat shop
in the world

00:59:52.422 --> 00:59:55.133
and one of the oldest
still family-owned businesses

00:59:55.216 --> 00:59:57.051
in the world as well.

00:59:57.093 --> 01:00:00.221
We originally opened on
the other side of the street.

01:00:00.263 --> 01:00:02.849
This is the sunny
side of the street.

01:00:02.932 --> 01:00:05.435
You always do more business
on the sunny side of the street,

01:00:05.476 --> 01:00:07.312
because that's
where people want to walk.

01:00:08.438 --> 01:00:10.356
My great, great, great,

01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:12.066
however many
great-grandfathers it is,

01:00:12.108 --> 01:00:14.110
was a gentleman
called James Benning

01:00:14.152 --> 01:00:17.864
and he is famous as he is
the original Mad Hatter

01:00:17.947 --> 01:00:19.741
that Lewis Carroll
based the character on

01:00:19.782 --> 01:00:21.659
in&lt;i&gt; Alice in Wonderland.&lt;/i&gt;

01:00:21.743 --> 01:00:25.496
So I am a direct descendant
of the original Mad Hatter.

01:00:25.580 --> 01:00:27.624
I'm very proud of that.

01:00:27.665 --> 01:00:30.209
We've got a great connection
with James Bond.

01:00:30.293 --> 01:00:32.629
We hatted
Sean Connery in the '60s.

01:00:32.712 --> 01:00:35.757
In&lt;i&gt; Dr. No,&lt;/i&gt;
he famously wore a Trilby.

01:00:35.798 --> 01:00:38.259
In&lt;i&gt; Goldfinger,&lt;/i&gt; Oddjob wore a,

01:00:38.301 --> 01:00:41.387
what we call
a square crown Coke

01:00:41.471 --> 01:00:43.640
and that was the one
with the reinforced brim,

01:00:43.681 --> 01:00:45.808
which he used to throw
at his victims

01:00:45.850 --> 01:00:48.519
and sliced their heads off
and all sorts.

01:00:48.603 --> 01:00:52.815
Famously, the square
crown Coke was also worn

01:00:52.857 --> 01:00:54.442
by Winston Churchill.

01:00:54.484 --> 01:00:56.527
We were very proud
that Winston Churchill

01:00:56.611 --> 01:00:58.112
was a big customer of ours,

01:00:58.154 --> 01:01:00.990
before and during
and after the war.

01:01:01.032 --> 01:01:04.327
When the war was
taking a turn for the worse,

01:01:04.369 --> 01:01:06.329
you'd find that
when you look in the ledgers,

01:01:06.412 --> 01:01:09.332
there'd be an insertion for
Winston Churchill for a new hat,

01:01:09.374 --> 01:01:11.626
because it was a way
of him lifting his spirits

01:01:11.668 --> 01:01:13.169
when the chips were down.

01:01:13.252 --> 01:01:16.255
One of the moments in history

01:01:16.339 --> 01:01:18.758
that my family
are very proud of,

01:01:18.841 --> 01:01:21.344
is that my grandfather
fitted the crown

01:01:21.386 --> 01:01:23.179
for the Queen's coronation.

01:01:23.221 --> 01:01:26.182
We were commissioned by
Garrards, the Queen's jewellers.

01:01:26.224 --> 01:01:30.228
to make a velvet pad
that went inside the crown,

01:01:30.311 --> 01:01:32.021
so it would size it down,

01:01:32.063 --> 01:01:35.483
so it would fit
the Queen on her coronation day.

01:01:35.525 --> 01:01:38.152
So we use a machine
called a conformateur,

01:01:38.194 --> 01:01:40.863
a French invention,
dates back to 1849

01:01:40.947 --> 01:01:43.825
and we still use
the same machine today.

01:01:43.866 --> 01:01:46.160
We haven't found
a better way of doing it.

01:01:46.202 --> 01:01:48.579
Looks like an instrument
of torture.

01:01:48.663 --> 01:01:51.541
I think someone described it
as quite steampunk

01:01:51.582 --> 01:01:53.334
and I quite like that.

01:01:53.376 --> 01:01:55.670
Right, okay, so if you
look straight ahead for me.

01:01:55.712 --> 01:01:59.590
Plonk the old girl on,
get it centred for you

01:01:59.674 --> 01:02:01.551
and then just pushes down.

01:02:01.592 --> 01:02:04.554
Looks like it should hurt,
but it doesn't hurt at all.

01:02:04.595 --> 01:02:07.014
And then we just
give that a squeeze.

01:02:09.600 --> 01:02:12.061
There we go, lift that off.

01:02:12.103 --> 01:02:13.896
It's little bit of an old girl,

01:02:13.980 --> 01:02:16.315
so we have to give it a little
bit of gentle persuasion.

01:02:16.399 --> 01:02:22.363
And then hopefully, there's
an imprint of your head shape.

01:02:22.405 --> 01:02:24.157
There you go, sir.

01:02:24.240 --> 01:02:26.451
That is amazing
and it's an exotic looking egg.

01:02:26.534 --> 01:02:27.952
Yes, yes.

01:02:30.079 --> 01:02:34.167
What I love is when
I get a customer coming in here,

01:02:34.250 --> 01:02:36.753
a young guy comes in and says,

01:02:36.794 --> 01:02:38.921
"I've always wanted a Lock hat

01:02:39.005 --> 01:02:41.758
"because my grandfather
wore one."

01:02:41.841 --> 01:02:46.095
[inspiring orchestral
instrumental]

01:02:46.137 --> 01:02:51.100
♪♪♪

01:03:15.374 --> 01:03:21.839
♪♪♪

01:03:51.911 --> 01:03:56.874
♪♪♪

01:04:10.513 --> 01:04:11.973
WILLIAM SKINNER:
Frank, you've been very patient

01:04:12.014 --> 01:04:13.933
for the various visits to here

01:04:14.016 --> 01:04:18.271
and today's visit is to
see the outfit completed now.

01:04:18.354 --> 01:04:22.316
I am very, very pleased
as how it's turned out.

01:04:32.201 --> 01:04:34.370
[horse loudly neighs]

01:04:36.914 --> 01:04:39.709
LORD PALMER: My son
being a racehorse trainer...

01:04:39.792 --> 01:04:43.546
I mean, he's had as many
as over 100 horses in training

01:04:43.629 --> 01:04:47.383
at any one time
and quite how he manages

01:04:47.466 --> 01:04:50.011
to know which horse
to run, in which race,

01:04:50.052 --> 01:04:52.221
in which country, I don't know.

01:04:52.305 --> 01:04:56.350
And of course,
a lot of staff here follow Hugo.

01:04:56.392 --> 01:04:58.936
Quite often. I'm stopped
in the passage and they go,

01:04:59.020 --> 01:05:01.355
"Hello, my Lord,
I'm 100 pounds better off

01:05:01.397 --> 01:05:04.442
than when I last saw you."

01:05:04.525 --> 01:05:07.361
&lt;i&gt;COMMENTATOR: ...won by&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;the favourite, Chauncery House!&lt;/i&gt;

01:05:07.403 --> 01:05:09.280
&lt;i&gt;Trained by Nicky Henderson.&lt;/i&gt;

01:05:09.363 --> 01:05:11.157
&lt;i&gt;Ridden by Nico De Boinville.&lt;/i&gt;

01:05:11.240 --> 01:05:13.409
We're here at Sandown Park,

01:05:13.492 --> 01:05:15.870
a quintessential
British racecourse.

01:05:15.912 --> 01:05:17.914
ALEX TRUESDALE: We try
and come as much as we can,

01:05:17.955 --> 01:05:20.207
we absolutely love horses.

01:05:20.249 --> 01:05:22.209
We have a share in
a couple of horses as well,

01:05:22.251 --> 01:05:23.586
so it's our favourite place
to be.

01:05:23.628 --> 01:05:25.421
Yeah.

01:05:25.463 --> 01:05:27.423
Sandown Park has always
been a favourite racecourse

01:05:27.465 --> 01:05:28.925
of the Royal Family.

01:05:28.966 --> 01:05:30.801
Indeed, the Queen Mother
used to say

01:05:30.885 --> 01:05:32.428
it was her favourite racecourse.

01:05:32.470 --> 01:05:34.096
And Nicky Henderson,
who trains for the Queen,

01:05:34.138 --> 01:05:35.932
is here today.

01:05:35.973 --> 01:05:38.059
I know people don't
like two-horse races.

01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:40.603
I love them,
in fact, one-horse races.

01:05:40.645 --> 01:05:42.813
[laughing]

01:05:44.941 --> 01:05:46.525
NICKY HENDERSON:
Concorde used to come over

01:05:46.609 --> 01:05:48.361
5 to 11 every morning.

01:05:48.444 --> 01:05:50.905
You could set your clock by it.

01:05:50.947 --> 01:05:52.740
Right, we're going again.

01:05:52.782 --> 01:05:56.160
I'm Nicky Henderson,
a racehorse trainer.

01:05:57.495 --> 01:06:01.123
I was always equestrian-minded,
if you like.

01:06:01.165 --> 01:06:04.543
Even from school days
and at Eton in those days,

01:06:04.627 --> 01:06:06.295
racing was about
the biggest crime.

01:06:06.337 --> 01:06:09.048
To be caught on a racecourse
was a heinous crime.

01:06:10.299 --> 01:06:12.385
Well, I was very lucky actually,

01:06:12.468 --> 01:06:14.303
because the Queen Mother's
horses came here

01:06:14.345 --> 01:06:16.138
and we had some wonderful times.

01:06:16.222 --> 01:06:19.475
I mean, she was
a spectacular lady.

01:06:19.558 --> 01:06:21.978
I think she
really enjoyed the people

01:06:22.019 --> 01:06:23.729
that were involved in racing.

01:06:23.813 --> 01:06:25.147
Racing's full of characters.

01:06:25.231 --> 01:06:27.650
Some good, some bad,
mostly good.

01:06:30.486 --> 01:06:33.447
The Queen took over
the Queen Mother's horses

01:06:33.489 --> 01:06:36.075
and all the mares
that were breeding.

01:06:36.158 --> 01:06:38.494
She toured the horses
and she loved to come down

01:06:38.536 --> 01:06:40.246
and see them.

01:06:40.329 --> 01:06:43.582
And it's lovely that she
takes such an interest in it all

01:06:43.666 --> 01:06:45.668
and as you know,

01:06:45.710 --> 01:06:49.880
racing is probably her number
one relaxation, hobby, passion.

01:06:51.716 --> 01:06:54.510
Both the Queen Mother
and the Queen, they're A,

01:06:54.552 --> 01:06:56.637
very knowledgeable, but B,

01:06:56.679 --> 01:07:00.141
terribly easy to work with
and talk to about something

01:07:00.182 --> 01:07:02.101
that we both love.

01:07:02.852 --> 01:07:05.938
The Gold Cup, the Champion
Hurdle, the Champion Chase,

01:07:06.022 --> 01:07:08.399
they are the pinnacle,
the Oscar, if you like,

01:07:08.482 --> 01:07:10.317
of the racing world.

01:07:10.359 --> 01:07:13.279
Over the years,
there've been some good days

01:07:13.362 --> 01:07:15.031
and I suppose these
are the special ones,

01:07:15.114 --> 01:07:18.409
because these are Bobs Worth
and Long Run's Gold Cups.

01:07:19.452 --> 01:07:24.373
2012 was a particularly
good Cheltenham festival.

01:07:24.457 --> 01:07:28.169
Horse's feet vary in size
quite considerably

01:07:28.210 --> 01:07:30.671
and as you can see,
there are big ones

01:07:30.713 --> 01:07:32.131
and there are little ones.

01:07:33.758 --> 01:07:36.677
This is actually a youngster
of Her Majesty's,

01:07:36.719 --> 01:07:38.512
called Rapid Flight.

01:07:38.554 --> 01:07:41.015
He's-- They're all homebred.

01:07:41.057 --> 01:07:43.976
And he's a rather sweet horse.

01:07:44.060 --> 01:07:48.064
He won the other day and he's
due to run again this weekend,

01:07:48.147 --> 01:07:50.066
if he doesn't eat the door.

01:07:50.149 --> 01:07:52.026
But he's just a nice youngster,

01:07:52.068 --> 01:07:55.571
his life's in front of him,
so who knows?

01:07:55.654 --> 01:07:57.907
And what is
the breakfast of champions?

01:07:58.616 --> 01:08:00.117
Well, this morning he-- What?

01:08:00.201 --> 01:08:01.911
-What he's actually had to eat?
-Yes.

01:08:01.952 --> 01:08:05.122
He's had a bowl of nuts,
he's had some hay,

01:08:05.206 --> 01:08:06.707
he's been out,

01:08:06.749 --> 01:08:08.584
he's been actually
jumping this morning.

01:08:09.543 --> 01:08:11.003
Wow, so beautiful.

01:08:11.087 --> 01:08:12.463
So, Rapid Flight.

01:08:12.546 --> 01:08:14.256
He's called Rapid Flight.

01:08:14.298 --> 01:08:17.343
I don't know how rapid he is,
but he won the other day

01:08:17.426 --> 01:08:19.261
and I hope you
win again this weekend.

01:08:26.018 --> 01:08:27.937
FRANK MANNION:
Have you ever been to the Proms?

01:08:28.020 --> 01:08:29.271
I haven't, no.

01:08:29.355 --> 01:08:31.232
[laughing]

01:08:31.273 --> 01:08:34.276
I'm not very British, am I?
[laughing]

01:08:34.318 --> 01:08:36.779
[light orchestral music]

01:08:36.821 --> 01:08:42.118
♪♪♪

01:08:42.159 --> 01:08:43.786
Could I tempt you
with a programme, sir?

01:08:43.828 --> 01:08:45.454
Oh my goodness me,
yes of course.

01:08:45.496 --> 01:08:48.457
Yes, it's £5 and it goes
to support our charities

01:08:48.541 --> 01:08:50.626
and we've just surpassed
a million pounds

01:08:50.668 --> 01:08:51.794
raised for charity.

01:08:52.628 --> 01:08:55.047
I'm Susie Gregson,
founder and Chief Executive

01:08:55.131 --> 01:08:56.590
of Proms at St Jude's.

01:08:56.632 --> 01:08:58.217
FRANK MANNION:
I think you're missing

01:08:58.300 --> 01:09:00.553
three very important letters
there, Susie.

01:09:01.637 --> 01:09:06.308
MBE. I was awarded an MBE
by Her Majesty, the Queen.

01:09:06.350 --> 01:09:09.979
I was awarded it
for services to the community.

01:09:10.020 --> 01:09:12.106
Pretty much for founding
Proms at St Jude's

01:09:12.148 --> 01:09:14.066
and for keeping it
going for so many years.

01:09:14.150 --> 01:09:19.029
♪♪♪

01:09:19.113 --> 01:09:21.657
Proms was set up
almost 30 years ago.

01:09:21.740 --> 01:09:23.492
I don't think
any of us anticipated

01:09:23.534 --> 01:09:25.161
that it would last
as long as it did

01:09:25.244 --> 01:09:26.787
and be as much loved as it is.

01:09:26.829 --> 01:09:29.665
By pulling together,
we've become a community

01:09:29.707 --> 01:09:31.417
and over the years,

01:09:31.500 --> 01:09:33.252
we've consistently
raised money for good causes.

01:09:33.335 --> 01:09:36.505
It's almost entirely
run by volunteers of all ages.

01:09:37.339 --> 01:09:39.008
Proms isn't a new concept.

01:09:39.091 --> 01:09:41.677
It started, I think,
in the mid-18th century

01:09:41.760 --> 01:09:44.013
and it started
with pleasure gardens,

01:09:44.054 --> 01:09:47.725
where people could stroll around
while musicians played.

01:09:48.934 --> 01:09:50.895
Well, tonight we've got
a wonderful concert

01:09:50.978 --> 01:09:53.606
with Braimah
and Konya Kanneh-Mason.

01:09:53.689 --> 01:09:56.275
The Kanneh-Masons will
be playing Elgar's&lt;i&gt; Sospiri&lt;/i&gt;

01:09:56.358 --> 01:09:58.068
and Elgar, of course,

01:09:58.152 --> 01:09:59.987
was actually living in
Hampstead when he wrote it.

01:10:00.029 --> 01:10:02.239
So really, we're playing it
on his doorstep.

01:10:02.865 --> 01:10:07.786
♪♪♪

01:10:28.724 --> 01:10:30.893
BRAIMAH KANNEH-MASON:
It was special to play a piece

01:10:30.935 --> 01:10:33.395
in Hampstead knowing
that it was written so close

01:10:33.437 --> 01:10:35.314
to where we were performing.

01:10:37.066 --> 01:10:38.859
We both live in West Hampstead.

01:10:38.901 --> 01:10:41.528
It was a really lovely
atmosphere for us to play in.

01:10:43.072 --> 01:10:44.865
If Elgar was sat
in the audience,

01:10:44.907 --> 01:10:47.243
I would love
to speak to him afterwards

01:10:47.326 --> 01:10:49.078
and ask how he thinks we did

01:10:49.119 --> 01:10:50.996
and what he thinks
we should do or change.

01:10:52.915 --> 01:10:54.458
I grew up with music
around the house.

01:10:54.541 --> 01:10:56.293
It was always there,

01:10:56.377 --> 01:10:58.295
so I think that's how I
naturally gravitated towards it,

01:10:58.379 --> 01:11:00.631
because then I was
just immersed in music

01:11:00.714 --> 01:11:02.132
from a very early age.

01:11:04.760 --> 01:11:06.887
BRAIMAH KANNEH-MASON:
We were extremely lucky

01:11:06.929 --> 01:11:09.932
to have parents
who grew up loving music.

01:11:09.974 --> 01:11:13.102
They really believed
that it was an essential part

01:11:13.143 --> 01:11:17.439
of the education, and we've
just been really, really lucky

01:11:17.481 --> 01:11:20.067
to have had
incredible opportunities

01:11:20.109 --> 01:11:23.362
and an amazing support network.

01:11:23.445 --> 01:11:25.406
[mournful violin and piano duet]

01:11:25.447 --> 01:11:30.244
♪♪♪

01:11:44.842 --> 01:11:46.593
DYLAN KAWENDE:
My name is Dylan Kawende.

01:11:46.635 --> 01:11:49.263
I'm the British-born son
of Congolese Rwandan refugees.

01:11:49.305 --> 01:11:52.308
I set up
the #GetDyl2Cambridge campaign

01:11:52.391 --> 01:11:55.019
to pursue my dream
of becoming a lawyer.

01:11:56.645 --> 01:11:59.815
So my dad received an offer to
study at Cambridge University,

01:11:59.857 --> 01:12:01.692
but he couldn't
take up the place

01:12:01.775 --> 01:12:03.527
because he didn't
have the funding.

01:12:03.610 --> 01:12:07.364
Back in the '90s,
my parents migrated to the UK

01:12:07.448 --> 01:12:09.992
during a time
of chaos and disorder.

01:12:10.034 --> 01:12:14.121
They always dreamed
of a better future for myself

01:12:14.163 --> 01:12:16.999
and my four siblings.
So when they came over here,

01:12:17.041 --> 01:12:20.169
the aspiration was that
we would all benefit society.

01:12:20.210 --> 01:12:23.088
And that has helped me
to forge my own path

01:12:23.172 --> 01:12:25.632
and despite
the financial hurdles

01:12:25.674 --> 01:12:27.301
of studying law at
Cambridge University,

01:12:27.343 --> 01:12:29.595
I've been able
to pursue my dream.

01:12:29.678 --> 01:12:31.805
Lots of people will
recognise my campaign,

01:12:31.847 --> 01:12:33.807
my #GetDyl2Cambridge campaign.

01:12:33.849 --> 01:12:36.101
An arduous effort,
but definitely worth it

01:12:36.185 --> 01:12:38.604
and I wouldn't have
had all these resources

01:12:38.687 --> 01:12:40.522
if not for
the people who believed in me.

01:12:40.564 --> 01:12:43.609
It's been an amazing journey,
an amazing experience.

01:12:51.784 --> 01:12:56.705
There is still that
feeling in rural England.

01:12:56.789 --> 01:12:59.917
There's something
rather lovely about it.

01:13:01.835 --> 01:13:06.924
I love going into old churches
in little villages

01:13:07.007 --> 01:13:13.389
and sensing the history and
the sense of peace and quiet.

01:13:14.556 --> 01:13:17.184
I love going to Grantchester

01:13:17.226 --> 01:13:20.896
and having cream tea
in the orchard tea rooms.

01:13:22.022 --> 01:13:25.192
Is that the whole
of modern Britain?

01:13:25.234 --> 01:13:28.404
No, of course not.
But it's part of the whole.

01:13:29.488 --> 01:13:33.909
IAN MCKELLEN: Chocolate box,
Christmas card, the village.

01:13:33.951 --> 01:13:35.744
I'm not a villager,

01:13:35.786 --> 01:13:38.080
I'm a provincial urban lad,

01:13:38.122 --> 01:13:40.082
so I get much more sentimental

01:13:40.165 --> 01:13:43.252
about the paintings
of L.S. Lowry.

01:13:43.293 --> 01:13:48.882
Lowry painted the now demolished
cityscapes of the North.

01:13:48.924 --> 01:13:50.926
The mean little houses
and streets

01:13:50.968 --> 01:13:53.720
and the big overpowering
factories and churches.

01:13:53.762 --> 01:13:55.264
Smoke in the air.

01:13:56.640 --> 01:13:58.809
Damp. Dirty.

01:14:00.477 --> 01:14:02.813
Well, the North
was a bit like that

01:14:02.896 --> 01:14:06.608
when I was growing up and I can
get sentimental about that,

01:14:06.650 --> 01:14:08.152
rather than...

01:14:10.404 --> 01:14:13.699
anything down at Grantchester
with all those posh people.

01:14:18.245 --> 01:14:19.830
You know, the mountains
of the Lake District,

01:14:19.913 --> 01:14:21.874
the mountains
of the Highlands of Scotland,

01:14:21.957 --> 01:14:24.960
they are some of the oldest
landmasses in the world.

01:14:25.002 --> 01:14:28.755
The Himalayas, the tall
mountains these days are new,

01:14:28.797 --> 01:14:30.215
they've just arrived.

01:14:31.633 --> 01:14:36.096
The Pennines have been
here since time immemorial

01:14:36.138 --> 01:14:39.141
and I get sentimental
about that.

01:14:39.183 --> 01:14:42.144
If I am trying to think
what it is to be British

01:14:42.227 --> 01:14:45.981
is to have lived in
the variable landscapes.

01:14:46.023 --> 01:14:49.568
Which people down South
here on this little island,

01:14:49.651 --> 01:14:52.279
people who live in London,
they don't know about mountains.

01:14:52.321 --> 01:14:54.573
There aren't any mountains here,
there're a few hills.

01:14:54.656 --> 01:14:56.366
No mountains.

01:15:01.538 --> 01:15:04.541
I don't think there's
a direct link between us

01:15:04.625 --> 01:15:08.337
today living in London and the
London where Shakespeare lived.

01:15:08.378 --> 01:15:11.006
I mean that's long, long ago
and it was very, very different.

01:15:11.048 --> 01:15:14.259
Very violent,
horrible place, actually.

01:15:14.343 --> 01:15:17.179
Cruelty on the streets.

01:15:17.221 --> 01:15:19.056
Deaths in the air.

01:15:19.139 --> 01:15:21.350
Short lives.

01:15:21.391 --> 01:15:24.144
Dirty. Disease-ridden.

01:15:24.186 --> 01:15:26.063
Mmm-mmm.

01:15:31.360 --> 01:15:33.362
BRIAN NEWMAN-SMITH: You're
at Bekonscot Model Village,

01:15:33.403 --> 01:15:35.489
the oldest model village
in the world.

01:15:40.702 --> 01:15:42.871
This is where we got
the quirky names.

01:15:42.913 --> 01:15:46.792
The fruiter is,
You Are A Peach, Heavenly Soles

01:15:46.875 --> 01:15:50.671
and this is the smallest
Marks &amp; Spencers in the world.

01:15:50.712 --> 01:15:52.339
What did they sell in the '30s?

01:15:52.381 --> 01:15:54.174
And they sent us
all the information.

01:15:54.216 --> 01:15:58.136
And basically they just
sold hats, stockings, shoes,

01:15:58.220 --> 01:15:59.972
gloves and scarves.

01:16:00.055 --> 01:16:02.558
Things like that.
Not like they do today.

01:16:05.227 --> 01:16:09.314
This is a little memorial to
our founder, Roland Callingham.

01:16:09.398 --> 01:16:11.900
People think he's buried there,
but he's not.

01:16:13.193 --> 01:16:15.988
FRANK MANNION: Why do you think
the 1930s was chosen

01:16:16.071 --> 01:16:17.656
as the period?

01:16:17.739 --> 01:16:19.575
I think it was
the time was just about right,

01:16:19.616 --> 01:16:22.744
because then after that,
everything sort of changed.

01:16:22.786 --> 01:16:24.997
I think we got it just right.

01:16:25.080 --> 01:16:27.249
FRANK MANNION: So it was
almost like an idyllic,

01:16:27.291 --> 01:16:28.875
quintessentially British time?

01:16:28.917 --> 01:16:31.712
Oh, I think so.
No doubt about that. Yes.

01:16:31.753 --> 01:16:34.172
Yes, it was. It was.

01:16:37.426 --> 01:16:39.261
That is English.

01:16:39.303 --> 01:16:42.931
Even now you go out and about
on a Saturday and Sunday,

01:16:42.973 --> 01:16:44.308
they're playing cricket.

01:16:53.108 --> 01:16:56.612
One thing we can't
get to scale are the fish.

01:16:56.653 --> 01:17:01.116
These are enormous,
mutant whales in scale, so yes.

01:17:01.199 --> 01:17:04.077
[toy train clicking]

01:17:04.119 --> 01:17:06.455
FRANK MANNION: Do you think
the trains run on time here?

01:17:06.496 --> 01:17:08.290
Oh, definitely.

01:17:08.332 --> 01:17:11.293
No leaves on the line
or anything like that, no.

01:17:12.961 --> 01:17:14.171
Hiya.

01:17:17.424 --> 01:17:19.885
FRANK MANNION: Why are
Pixar movies so successful?

01:17:19.968 --> 01:17:23.096
Because Pixar works for kids
and it works for adults.

01:17:23.138 --> 01:17:26.224
Maybe that's the secret
success formula for Bekonscot.

01:17:26.308 --> 01:17:28.143
BRIAN NEWMAN-SMITH:
Yes, I think it is, yes.

01:17:28.185 --> 01:17:30.729
You come as a child,
you bring your children,

01:17:30.812 --> 01:17:34.191
you then bring the grandchildren
and it starts all over again.

01:17:36.193 --> 01:17:40.155
And you've got Ascot Racecourse,
as it was in the 1930s.

01:17:40.197 --> 01:17:42.658
One of our model makers
who got the MBE,

01:17:42.741 --> 01:17:44.159
he went up to meet the Queen.

01:17:44.201 --> 01:17:45.911
He actually spoke to the Queen

01:17:45.994 --> 01:17:49.498
for it seems a lot longer
than everyone else,

01:17:49.581 --> 01:17:52.668
because she said, "Oh,
I came such and such a year."

01:17:52.751 --> 01:17:54.795
And he said, "Excuse me, Mom,
you came in," whatever.

01:17:54.836 --> 01:17:56.171
And they chatted, you know.

01:17:56.213 --> 01:17:57.631
If it'd been anyone else,

01:17:57.673 --> 01:17:59.424
it'd been off
with his head, you know.

01:17:59.508 --> 01:18:05.347
Her Majesty came with
Queen Mary in '34 and '39.

01:18:05.430 --> 01:18:09.518
The Royal Family has decided
that it will define itself

01:18:09.601 --> 01:18:13.897
through its theatrical
presentation of itself.

01:18:14.898 --> 01:18:17.067
And a parade, a royal parade,

01:18:17.150 --> 01:18:20.696
is something quite remarkable
and it's very British.

01:18:22.656 --> 01:18:25.909
This is the first time
we've had an opportunity

01:18:25.992 --> 01:18:28.787
to celebrate a Platinum Jubilee.

01:18:28.870 --> 01:18:31.581
The first time
in all our history

01:18:31.665 --> 01:18:34.751
that somebody should be
on the throne for 70 years.

01:18:34.835 --> 01:18:37.629
How fantastic is that?

01:18:37.713 --> 01:18:40.298
Without a doubt,
over the last few years,

01:18:40.382 --> 01:18:44.302
the most extraordinary person,
not just who is British,

01:18:44.386 --> 01:18:46.388
is of course,
Her Majesty the Queen.

01:18:46.471 --> 01:18:48.306
What an amazing woman.

01:18:48.390 --> 01:18:51.226
Well, she means the glue
that holds the nation together.

01:18:51.309 --> 01:18:52.853
Because if you look back,

01:18:52.894 --> 01:18:54.730
she's been one
of the most effective

01:18:54.813 --> 01:18:56.732
and best monarchs
we've ever had.

01:18:56.815 --> 01:19:00.068
The Queen means everything.
She's iconic.

01:19:00.152 --> 01:19:03.071
She is an incredible role model.

01:19:03.113 --> 01:19:05.073
She's been
a fantastic public servant.

01:19:05.157 --> 01:19:08.201
The sport of kings, it has to
be renamed the sport of queens.

01:19:08.243 --> 01:19:10.203
She's a star.
Like you know,

01:19:10.245 --> 01:19:13.415
Queen is someone
everyone has to look up to.

01:19:13.457 --> 01:19:14.916
Oh, I love the Queen.

01:19:14.958 --> 01:19:16.460
We'll raise
a glass to the Queen.

01:19:16.543 --> 01:19:18.545
Yeah, without
the Queen being here,

01:19:18.587 --> 01:19:20.005
this country
wouldn't be what it is.

01:19:20.088 --> 01:19:21.590
Exactly.

01:19:21.631 --> 01:19:22.924
FRANK MANNION:
But the Royal Family,

01:19:22.966 --> 01:19:24.384
aren't they German in origin?

01:19:24.426 --> 01:19:25.594
We don't-- Well...

01:19:25.635 --> 01:19:27.012
We don't look at them like that.

01:19:27.095 --> 01:19:28.555
No. No.

01:19:28.597 --> 01:19:30.307
They've become British,

01:19:30.390 --> 01:19:31.975
I'm not sure whether
one would call them English.

01:19:32.058 --> 01:19:33.477
Oh, they're English,
they're not German,

01:19:33.560 --> 01:19:35.228
they are Anglo-Saxon.

01:19:35.270 --> 01:19:37.063
Well, where did
the Anglo-Saxons come from?

01:19:37.105 --> 01:19:40.192
The reason they
are very British,

01:19:40.275 --> 01:19:42.944
is that they are very British.

01:19:44.362 --> 01:19:46.281
They're hugely valued
for what they offer

01:19:46.364 --> 01:19:48.074
and for what they represent.

01:19:49.993 --> 01:19:52.954
The monarch has
to be apolitical.

01:19:52.996 --> 01:19:55.791
But it doesn't
stop us, the world,

01:19:55.874 --> 01:19:58.627
the papers analysing, perhaps,

01:19:58.710 --> 01:20:03.465
some moments that seem
slightly more provocative.

01:20:03.507 --> 01:20:06.760
Her most marked
sartorial choices,

01:20:06.802 --> 01:20:10.138
was at the State Opening
of Parliament in 2017.

01:20:10.222 --> 01:20:14.351
She came in steadfast
in a beautiful mid-blue suit,

01:20:14.434 --> 01:20:16.603
matching hat with a perhaps

01:20:16.645 --> 01:20:19.940
not too subtle
circular formation

01:20:19.981 --> 01:20:23.401
of star-like yellow flowers,
resembling the European flag.

01:20:23.485 --> 01:20:25.654
Part of me thinks if
she was allowed to talk,

01:20:25.695 --> 01:20:27.656
she would be disappointed

01:20:27.697 --> 01:20:30.367
that we were
breaking our ties with Europe,

01:20:30.450 --> 01:20:33.537
but only she knows
and maybe her dresser.

01:20:33.620 --> 01:20:39.501
♪♪♪

01:20:50.011 --> 01:20:51.847
I was 20-years-old.

01:20:51.930 --> 01:20:54.933
I remember all the people
in the street, myself included,

01:20:55.016 --> 01:20:56.852
to see the car and the Queen.

01:20:56.893 --> 01:20:58.687
Everybody was amazed by that.

01:20:58.770 --> 01:21:00.605
That was an amazing day.

01:21:00.689 --> 01:21:02.190
FRANK MANNION:
So you saw the Queen go by?

01:21:02.274 --> 01:21:03.942
Absolutely.

01:21:04.025 --> 01:21:05.318
FRANK MANNION:
Were there are George's crosses

01:21:05.360 --> 01:21:06.528
on the streets then?

01:21:06.570 --> 01:21:07.988
Oh, that's a good question.

01:21:08.029 --> 01:21:10.282
I don't remember that.
[laughing]

01:21:10.365 --> 01:21:13.869
As a country, we're pretty
good at friendly appropriation

01:21:13.910 --> 01:21:16.204
of other people's property.

01:21:16.246 --> 01:21:17.956
I mean, St. George,

01:21:18.039 --> 01:21:19.749
I don't think St. George
was actually an Englishman.

01:21:19.833 --> 01:21:21.793
I don't know too much about him.

01:21:21.877 --> 01:21:24.212
I'm pretty sure he is the saint,

01:21:24.254 --> 01:21:27.674
the personification of Britain.

01:21:27.716 --> 01:21:29.718
So, yeah.

01:21:29.759 --> 01:21:32.137
The Genoese in
the north of Italy,

01:21:32.220 --> 01:21:35.724
their state flag
was the white and red flag

01:21:35.765 --> 01:21:38.184
that we know is
the English flag.

01:21:38.226 --> 01:21:40.103
JERMAINE JENAS:
From a footballer perspective,

01:21:40.186 --> 01:21:42.355
you know, we look at that
flag with real pride

01:21:42.397 --> 01:21:44.149
and as something to fight for

01:21:44.232 --> 01:21:46.151
and as something that we
want to kind of take across

01:21:46.234 --> 01:21:47.736
and do the best we could.

01:21:47.777 --> 01:21:49.571
So I always had
a lot of love and pride

01:21:49.613 --> 01:21:50.989
towards St George's Cross.

01:21:51.990 --> 01:21:54.409
I'm very pleased to have
a conversation with you guys,

01:21:54.451 --> 01:21:56.161
because you asked
me a good question.

01:21:56.244 --> 01:21:58.914
Why there are very
little Italian flag here

01:21:58.955 --> 01:22:01.041
and there is
also St George flag?

01:22:01.082 --> 01:22:04.544
St George's flag was
adopted in Genoa immediately

01:22:04.586 --> 01:22:07.088
after the first crusade.

01:22:07.130 --> 01:22:10.675
The King of England
decided to ask the permission

01:22:10.759 --> 01:22:13.094
to the Genoa Republic
to use the Genoa flag

01:22:13.178 --> 01:22:16.222
in order to be safe
cruising in Mediterranean area.

01:22:16.264 --> 01:22:19.559
Our so-called English flag,

01:22:19.601 --> 01:22:21.561
we were actually
shelling out the reddies

01:22:21.603 --> 01:22:23.188
year by year saying,

01:22:23.271 --> 01:22:25.023
"Hello Genoa, sorry,
we owe you a bit of money

01:22:25.106 --> 01:22:27.108
"for our national flag,
here you are, darling."

01:22:27.150 --> 01:22:31.696
I was not able to understand
why they stopped in 1599.

01:22:31.780 --> 01:22:34.157
Our King or Queen said,
"I think we've had enough

01:22:34.240 --> 01:22:36.993
"of paying out for this Genoese
flag and I like the flag,

01:22:37.077 --> 01:22:38.161
"let's keep it."

01:22:38.244 --> 01:22:40.789
So this is really our flag.

01:22:40.830 --> 01:22:44.042
We wrote down a letter to the
Queen, to Buckingham Palace.

01:22:44.125 --> 01:22:47.003
We received a very good letter
back from Buckingham Palace,

01:22:47.087 --> 01:22:49.339
I'm very proud
of that, saying, "Big thanks."

01:22:49.422 --> 01:22:52.968
Maybe one day with all
the money coming from the flag,

01:22:53.009 --> 01:22:55.387
we may reconstruct
the Republic of Genoa.

01:22:55.470 --> 01:22:56.930
That could be a good idea.

01:22:56.972 --> 01:22:58.932
Don't say here,
because this is illegal,

01:22:58.974 --> 01:23:01.977
so I'm not going to say that.
Okay.

01:23:02.060 --> 01:23:05.313
We need to have a conversation
about the St George flag.

01:23:05.355 --> 01:23:07.983
We love it
and I'm sure you guys love too.

01:23:08.024 --> 01:23:10.944
We can decide that April 23rd

01:23:10.986 --> 01:23:13.446
we should have
a celebration all together.

01:23:13.488 --> 01:23:16.116
People from England
and people from Genoa.

01:23:17.325 --> 01:23:19.577
FRANK MANNION: I, before
starting this documentary,

01:23:19.661 --> 01:23:22.038
thought that
the St George's flag

01:23:22.122 --> 01:23:24.708
is a quintessentially
English flag,

01:23:24.791 --> 01:23:26.292
but filming this documentary,

01:23:26.334 --> 01:23:28.753
we discovered that
the English stole it

01:23:28.837 --> 01:23:31.506
from the Italians,
from the Genoese.

01:23:31.548 --> 01:23:34.634
Well, to be honest,
it doesn't really surprise me.

01:23:34.676 --> 01:23:40.265
We're quite
a thievery nation, I suppose.

01:23:40.348 --> 01:23:42.058
Is that so?

01:23:42.142 --> 01:23:44.310
I'm not surprised
to learn that as a story.

01:23:44.352 --> 01:23:46.604
What you've just told me
is all brand new information,

01:23:46.688 --> 01:23:48.231
but very interesting
that maybe comes up

01:23:48.314 --> 01:23:49.941
in a quiz one day I might use.

01:23:52.902 --> 01:23:54.612
What is actually British?

01:23:54.696 --> 01:23:58.033
Think about it,
fish and chips isn't, tea isn't.

01:23:58.074 --> 01:24:01.703
A cup of tea,
"Cuppa cha, sir, cuppa cha."

01:24:01.786 --> 01:24:03.621
Lovely Cockney, "Cuppa cha."

01:24:03.705 --> 01:24:05.540
It's not
a Cockney word at all, "cha."

01:24:05.623 --> 01:24:08.668
"Cha" is
a Mandarin Chinese word.

01:24:08.710 --> 01:24:10.336
Tea is from China.

01:24:10.378 --> 01:24:12.338
Yes so, I think that's something

01:24:12.380 --> 01:24:15.675
to link the two cultures
together.

01:24:15.717 --> 01:24:18.178
FRANK MANNION: Three times
the number of British people

01:24:18.219 --> 01:24:20.847
have an Indian dish every week.

01:24:20.889 --> 01:24:24.184
Wow, that's great, okay.

01:24:24.225 --> 01:24:26.895
Which dish in Indian,
like, what do they like?

01:24:26.936 --> 01:24:29.064
FRANK MANNION:
They say chicken tikka masala.

01:24:29.105 --> 01:24:31.274
Wow, chicken tikka masala, nice.

01:24:31.357 --> 01:24:34.027
That's my favourite too.
[laughing]

01:24:34.069 --> 01:24:36.279
FRANK MANNION: Juana,
what's the national dish?

01:24:36.362 --> 01:24:38.990
Well, we definitely at
home still have fish and chips

01:24:39.074 --> 01:24:41.493
every Friday evening from
the local fish and chip shop.

01:24:41.576 --> 01:24:43.495
Fish and chips.

01:24:43.578 --> 01:24:48.041
That is the quintessential
English dish.

01:24:48.083 --> 01:24:51.753
Thank you very much to those
Jewish immigrants from Europe,

01:24:51.795 --> 01:24:54.547
who brought the idea
of fried fish with them.

01:24:54.589 --> 01:24:58.093
Beef, of course,
is a French word,&lt;i&gt; boeuf.&lt;/i&gt;

01:24:58.176 --> 01:25:01.096
Mutton and lamb
comes from the Normans.

01:25:01.179 --> 01:25:03.681
Oh and thank you
very much to the Vikings,

01:25:03.765 --> 01:25:05.266
because they brought this idea

01:25:05.308 --> 01:25:07.435
of smoking fish
and preserving fish,

01:25:07.519 --> 01:25:10.730
so smoked salmon
and smoked haddock and kippers.

01:25:10.772 --> 01:25:12.607
Thank you very
much to the Vikings.

01:25:12.690 --> 01:25:17.737
Pork, not pig, pork.

01:25:17.779 --> 01:25:21.574
Foreign word. Because the French
were better cooks.

01:25:21.616 --> 01:25:23.284
That's the reason.

01:25:23.326 --> 01:25:25.954
Imagine the UK without
any Caribbean influence.

01:25:25.995 --> 01:25:28.665
Rum punch,
jerk chicken, Red Stripe,

01:25:28.748 --> 01:25:32.293
like, a British person,
one of their favourite beers

01:25:32.335 --> 01:25:34.420
is generally a Red Stripe,
everyone knows it.

01:25:34.462 --> 01:25:35.964
Beer. English bitter beer,

01:25:36.005 --> 01:25:37.507
one of the most famous
in the world.

01:25:37.590 --> 01:25:38.967
That's not English.

01:25:39.008 --> 01:25:40.885
500 years ago,
we didn't have any hops.

01:25:40.969 --> 01:25:42.887
They came from the Netherlands,

01:25:42.971 --> 01:25:45.473
from what is now
Belgium and Holland.

01:25:45.515 --> 01:25:46.724
They had hops.

01:25:46.808 --> 01:25:49.060
They showed us how to make beer.

01:25:49.144 --> 01:25:51.521
Cider, cider,
English cider's lovely.

01:25:51.604 --> 01:25:53.148
Thank you, the Normans.

01:25:53.189 --> 01:25:54.816
Thank you,
William the Conqueror,

01:25:54.858 --> 01:25:56.568
for bringing
all your apples over

01:25:56.651 --> 01:25:58.278
and showing us
how to make cider.

01:25:58.319 --> 01:26:02.657
Whiskey. Now,
that'll be British. Would it?

01:26:02.699 --> 01:26:04.576
Why don't you ask the Irish?

01:26:04.659 --> 01:26:09.789
Because it's probably
the Irish who invented whiskey.

01:26:10.874 --> 01:26:15.461
Gin. Now gin, the quintessential
English drink.

01:26:15.503 --> 01:26:16.671
No, it isn't.

01:26:16.713 --> 01:26:18.506
We got the idea from Holland

01:26:18.590 --> 01:26:22.093
and they got the idea
from North Africa via Italy.

01:26:22.177 --> 01:26:23.511
Not English at all.

01:26:24.679 --> 01:26:27.974
Where did the sausage come from?
The British sausage.

01:26:28.016 --> 01:26:30.518
You can't get those abroad.

01:26:30.560 --> 01:26:32.437
And there's nothing like one.

01:26:32.520 --> 01:26:34.189
A British sausage.

01:26:34.856 --> 01:26:36.649
Superior to any sausage
I've ever had

01:26:36.691 --> 01:26:38.359
anywhere else in the world.

01:26:39.485 --> 01:26:42.780
Now, is that not
quintessentially British?

01:26:42.864 --> 01:26:47.493
♪♪♪

01:26:47.535 --> 01:26:49.704
BRIAN WALDY: Who are really
English is very hard to say,

01:26:49.746 --> 01:26:51.539
really, if you
look at the history.

01:26:51.581 --> 01:26:53.499
The more you look back,
the more interesting it gets

01:26:53.541 --> 01:26:56.169
and the more different peoples
come into the equation.

01:26:56.211 --> 01:26:58.963
It's so fascinating to
see one wave of immigrants

01:26:59.047 --> 01:27:00.506
after another so--

01:27:00.548 --> 01:27:02.467
And it's still
continuing of course.

01:27:02.550 --> 01:27:07.722
We've had to face our, you know,
all our phobias, haven't we?

01:27:07.805 --> 01:27:11.893
Our racisms and our fears and
our anxieties about the other,

01:27:11.935 --> 01:27:13.478
but we're all the other.

01:27:13.561 --> 01:27:15.396
We are the other.
Of course, we're mongrels.

01:27:15.480 --> 01:27:17.649
That's what's so
absolutely wonderful.

01:27:17.732 --> 01:27:20.735
PROFESSOR MICHAEL WHEELER:
This has, in some ways,

01:27:20.818 --> 01:27:23.404
always been
a multicultural society.

01:27:23.488 --> 01:27:25.365
It's more evident now,

01:27:25.406 --> 01:27:27.575
because of obviously
the end of empire

01:27:27.617 --> 01:27:30.245
and the whole impact
of a Commonwealth

01:27:30.286 --> 01:27:31.871
and all of those things.

01:27:31.913 --> 01:27:33.790
It's been very
interesting to see

01:27:33.873 --> 01:27:36.834
how many people have been
very keen to live in Britain.

01:27:36.918 --> 01:27:40.505
Is it just about there
being a national health system

01:27:40.588 --> 01:27:43.675
and there being
a social security system,

01:27:43.758 --> 01:27:46.094
of a kind that
you certainly don't get

01:27:46.135 --> 01:27:47.679
in the United States?

01:27:47.762 --> 01:27:49.472
No, it's about more than that.

01:27:49.555 --> 01:27:52.600
It's about
people being attracted

01:27:52.642 --> 01:27:57.605
to certain assumptions
about the way we live.

01:27:59.440 --> 01:28:01.359
I come--
My family come from Ireland.

01:28:01.442 --> 01:28:06.030
We know all about wanting
to be accepted as foreigners

01:28:06.114 --> 01:28:07.949
in this country.

01:28:08.032 --> 01:28:11.577
It takes a while,
but we do integrate,

01:28:11.619 --> 01:28:14.289
we do welcome people
from abroad.

01:28:14.330 --> 01:28:15.748
We always have.

01:28:15.790 --> 01:28:17.375
Churchill?

01:28:17.458 --> 01:28:19.877
-He was half American.
-FRANK MANNION: Hm?

01:28:19.961 --> 01:28:22.547
He was half--
His mother was American.

01:28:22.630 --> 01:28:25.133
Well, maybe that's
quintessentially British

01:28:25.174 --> 01:28:26.968
that in fact
you're not local at all,

01:28:27.051 --> 01:28:31.472
you're an invader
or an immigrant

01:28:31.556 --> 01:28:33.975
or started from
somewhere else, why not?

01:28:35.518 --> 01:28:39.397
I love the idea of this country
being multiracial,

01:28:39.480 --> 01:28:41.607
which it gloriously is.

01:28:41.649 --> 01:28:43.693
In an ideal world,
I like the sense

01:28:43.776 --> 01:28:45.528
that anybody could
consider themselves to...

01:28:45.611 --> 01:28:47.864
call Britain home,
because I think that its--

01:28:47.947 --> 01:28:54.370
At its best when it celebrates
how cosmopolitan it is.

01:28:54.454 --> 01:28:57.248
I get frustrated
when British people think

01:28:57.332 --> 01:29:01.502
we're not inclusive and that
we're not all embracing,

01:29:01.544 --> 01:29:04.464
because from what I've seen
from my travels, is that we are.

01:29:04.505 --> 01:29:06.174
We really are.

01:29:06.257 --> 01:29:07.675
FRANK MANNION:
Your surname is Petrucci.

01:29:07.759 --> 01:29:09.344
Mm-hm.

01:29:09.385 --> 01:29:10.845
FRANK MANNION:
Is that an English surname?

01:29:10.887 --> 01:29:12.972
It's Italian. Sicilian.
My Grandad.

01:29:13.014 --> 01:29:18.144
Launer is
a truly British company

01:29:18.186 --> 01:29:20.146
and I'm very proud of the fact,

01:29:20.188 --> 01:29:25.109
because of course my background
was originally from Germany.

01:29:25.193 --> 01:29:29.280
Jacob Dege came
over from Germany in 1855.

01:29:29.364 --> 01:29:33.868
My great-great-grandfather
was also a tailor in London.

01:29:33.910 --> 01:29:37.663
His son and Mr. Dege's,
or now it's pronounced Dege,

01:29:37.705 --> 01:29:39.916
their two sons got on very well,

01:29:39.999 --> 01:29:42.543
so they actually
started up on their own.

01:29:42.585 --> 01:29:45.838
Culturally, I think
because of the richness

01:29:45.880 --> 01:29:49.425
of people's experiences
from the Caribbean, from India,

01:29:49.509 --> 01:29:53.096
from Africa, from Europe
obviously makes it far richer

01:29:53.179 --> 01:29:55.306
and there is really
something for everyone.

01:29:56.516 --> 01:29:59.685
Oh, we are in
Chinatown, phenomenonal.

01:29:59.727 --> 01:30:02.188
Amazing place, yes.

01:30:02.230 --> 01:30:05.108
It's a good place to mix
with British culture

01:30:05.191 --> 01:30:07.902
and international
culture and Oriental,

01:30:07.985 --> 01:30:09.737
Chinese culture as well.

01:30:09.821 --> 01:30:11.739
There's so many
Indians over here

01:30:11.781 --> 01:30:14.075
and I don't feel
like I'm an outsider.

01:30:14.117 --> 01:30:15.910
I feel like
I belong to this place.

01:30:15.993 --> 01:30:19.497
British Asians are definitely
a huge part of Britain

01:30:19.580 --> 01:30:23.084
with our cuisines, with
our commerce, with our cricket.

01:30:23.126 --> 01:30:26.212
There are a lot
of dishes in our culture

01:30:26.254 --> 01:30:28.714
to have worth celebrating that.

01:30:28.756 --> 01:30:31.259
JAMIE HARRIS:
When I was retraining my career

01:30:31.300 --> 01:30:33.177
in horticulture, Leonardslee
was kind of spoken of

01:30:33.261 --> 01:30:35.596
in hushed terms then,
it was the lost hidden gem,

01:30:35.680 --> 01:30:37.849
it was shut for, you know,
eight to ten years.

01:30:37.932 --> 01:30:40.101
Ironically,
for a quintessentially English

01:30:40.143 --> 01:30:41.769
woodland garden,

01:30:41.811 --> 01:30:43.729
it was saved by
a South African-born family.

01:30:43.771 --> 01:30:46.190
So Penny Streeter
and her family bought it,

01:30:46.274 --> 01:30:48.067
reopened it
and are driving it forward,

01:30:48.109 --> 01:30:49.777
making it what it is today.

01:30:49.819 --> 01:30:51.487
Elgar was--

01:30:51.571 --> 01:30:53.406
He was often considered
quintessentially English,

01:30:53.448 --> 01:30:56.492
but a lot of his influences
were European.

01:30:56.576 --> 01:30:59.036
It's not unusual
for foreign influences

01:30:59.120 --> 01:31:01.831
to find their way into British
culture or British society.

01:31:01.914 --> 01:31:03.624
Same is true of cricket.

01:31:03.666 --> 01:31:06.127
It probably originated
in South East England,

01:31:06.169 --> 01:31:08.671
but there is a competing theory

01:31:08.754 --> 01:31:11.424
which suggests the game
was imported by Flemish weavers,

01:31:11.466 --> 01:31:14.802
who arrived in this country
in the 16th and 17th century.

01:31:14.886 --> 01:31:17.305
So, foreign influences
have made their way

01:31:17.346 --> 01:31:19.098
into English cricket,
just as they have

01:31:19.140 --> 01:31:21.476
into every other aspect
of British life.

01:31:21.517 --> 01:31:26.105
Britishness, for me,
is not about being insular.

01:31:26.147 --> 01:31:28.441
It's about being
open to the world,

01:31:28.483 --> 01:31:33.321
absorbing influences and being
able, in turn, to influence.

01:31:33.404 --> 01:31:36.407
And that, I think,
is one of the things

01:31:36.491 --> 01:31:38.868
that makes us special.

01:31:45.124 --> 01:31:47.960
PROFESSOR MICHAEL WHEELER:
Britain, 300 years old,

01:31:48.002 --> 01:31:51.047
England, 1,100 years old.

01:31:51.130 --> 01:31:53.925
They're very
different things historically.

01:31:54.008 --> 01:31:55.551
They often say about England

01:31:55.635 --> 01:31:57.678
that it's really the history
of the shires.

01:31:57.720 --> 01:32:02.517
Back in the mists of time is
where we come from, as it were.

01:32:04.560 --> 01:32:06.687
It's very interesting
that the British

01:32:06.729 --> 01:32:08.564
are often called the English,

01:32:08.648 --> 01:32:12.193
which extremely annoys members
of the other nations.

01:32:12.276 --> 01:32:15.321
Now where I come
from is extremely English.

01:32:15.363 --> 01:32:18.282
On all sides
of my family, English.

01:32:18.366 --> 01:32:20.034
FRANK MANNION:
You have Irish ancestry as well.

01:32:20.076 --> 01:32:21.536
I do.

01:32:21.577 --> 01:32:22.954
FRANK MANNION:
Do you consider yourself

01:32:23.037 --> 01:32:24.664
quintessentially
English or British?

01:32:24.705 --> 01:32:26.290
[chuckling]

01:32:26.374 --> 01:32:27.875
It's very interesting
you should ask that,

01:32:27.917 --> 01:32:29.877
because I've just
done that programme

01:32:29.919 --> 01:32:31.546
&lt;i&gt;Who Do You Think You Are?&lt;/i&gt;

01:32:31.587 --> 01:32:36.551
There is a huge slice of me
that is Irish.

01:32:36.634 --> 01:32:39.345
And I was born in England.

01:32:39.387 --> 01:32:42.348
And then there's a lot
of something elsewhere, as well.

01:32:42.390 --> 01:32:44.350
I can't say anymore.

01:32:44.392 --> 01:32:46.102
I'm like a kind of fruitcake.

01:32:46.185 --> 01:32:47.687
IAN MCKELLEN:
Yes, and I discovered

01:32:47.728 --> 01:32:49.605
when I did the same program

01:32:49.689 --> 01:32:51.649
that I had
quite a recent relative

01:32:51.732 --> 01:32:54.068
who was a professional actor.

01:32:54.110 --> 01:32:56.112
I could feel myself
to be a Kiwi,

01:32:56.195 --> 01:32:57.905
having worked there
for quite a lot.

01:32:57.947 --> 01:33:01.367
Sean, born and bred
in Swansea in South Wales

01:33:01.409 --> 01:33:03.244
and lived in
London most of his life,

01:33:03.286 --> 01:33:06.080
still feels he's abroad
when he's in London.

01:33:06.122 --> 01:33:08.666
I think I've brought
my Welshness with me

01:33:08.749 --> 01:33:10.918
throughout my whole life,
everywhere I've been.

01:33:10.960 --> 01:33:13.588
But I've lived in South Africa,
I've lived in America,

01:33:13.629 --> 01:33:16.382
I've lived in England, so
I've lived all over the world.

01:33:16.424 --> 01:33:17.925
And if you ask me in ten years,

01:33:18.009 --> 01:33:20.553
I'll probably be clearer,
but at the moment,

01:33:20.595 --> 01:33:23.264
I'm certainly
feeling very Welsh, yes.

01:33:23.306 --> 01:33:25.057
Blood wise, I'm half Irish,

01:33:25.099 --> 01:33:29.854
so I feel like
I'm a bit of everything.

01:33:29.937 --> 01:33:32.773
But I guess
I quite enjoy playing up

01:33:32.857 --> 01:33:35.443
to being a little bit
of a stereotype of somebody

01:33:35.526 --> 01:33:38.112
who's English, but you know,
and sort of sounding posh,

01:33:38.154 --> 01:33:39.739
but I'm not really posh either.

01:33:39.780 --> 01:33:42.116
I just happen to have
a posh voice.

01:33:42.158 --> 01:33:44.285
But my parents are
from South East London,

01:33:44.327 --> 01:33:45.661
so they talk like that.

01:33:45.745 --> 01:33:47.330
So I don't know what I am,

01:33:47.413 --> 01:33:49.165
but I quite enjoy
playing a bit of a character.

01:33:49.248 --> 01:33:50.833
And if I'm really feeling it,

01:33:50.916 --> 01:33:52.418
I like to pretend
I'm in&lt;i&gt; Downton Abbey.&lt;/i&gt;

01:33:52.460 --> 01:33:54.629
That's usually when I'm at home.

01:33:54.670 --> 01:33:57.256
Ooh, quintessentially British,
I think. Yes.

01:33:57.298 --> 01:33:59.342
Yes, I'd like to think.

01:33:59.425 --> 01:34:01.969
I think British is probably how
I would identify myself with,

01:34:02.011 --> 01:34:03.596
purely and simply because

01:34:03.638 --> 01:34:05.598
of the diverse nature
of where I'm from.

01:34:05.640 --> 01:34:08.059
You know, my dad's from Jamaica,
my mum's from England and Wales

01:34:08.142 --> 01:34:10.061
and you know
I think to encapsulate

01:34:10.144 --> 01:34:11.520
as many of the islands

01:34:11.604 --> 01:34:13.773
as I possibly can
and areas within myself,

01:34:13.814 --> 01:34:15.524
that's why I would
probably call myself British

01:34:15.608 --> 01:34:16.984
over just English.

01:34:17.068 --> 01:34:19.403
English. Yes.

01:34:19.487 --> 01:34:20.988
FRANK MANNION: Why?

01:34:21.072 --> 01:34:25.159
Um... I don't really know,
to be honest.

01:34:25.201 --> 01:34:31.415
British is a bit, maybe,
sensitive at the moment.

01:34:31.499 --> 01:34:33.000
-English.
-English.

01:34:33.084 --> 01:34:35.086
FRANK MANNION:
And why so decisively?

01:34:35.169 --> 01:34:38.130
Why? Because that's our origin.
We are English.

01:34:38.172 --> 01:34:40.341
Yep, definitely.

01:34:40.424 --> 01:34:45.137
I feel I completely identify
as a very British person.

01:34:45.179 --> 01:34:48.015
Yes, I'm very much a proud Brit.
Absolutely.

01:34:49.058 --> 01:34:52.395
I'm from Monmouthshire,
the border of Wales,

01:34:52.478 --> 01:34:55.815
so I think
I always sounded very English

01:34:55.856 --> 01:34:58.401
to the Welsh people
and Welsh to the English people.

01:34:58.484 --> 01:34:59.860
So I think I've always had

01:34:59.902 --> 01:35:01.612
a slightly mongrelised
accent, so--

01:35:01.696 --> 01:35:04.240
You're not born British,
you have it thrust upon you.

01:35:05.157 --> 01:35:07.368
I would consider myself English,

01:35:07.410 --> 01:35:10.579
though my parents
were both from Glasgow.

01:35:10.663 --> 01:35:14.458
Yes, I think I do consider
myself quintessentially English.

01:35:14.542 --> 01:35:17.461
I would consider myself British.

01:35:17.545 --> 01:35:20.005
The connotations
of being considered English

01:35:20.047 --> 01:35:22.299
are fairly negative,
I still feel.

01:35:22.383 --> 01:35:25.803
I am English, I am British,
I am Congolese, I am Rwandan,

01:35:25.886 --> 01:35:28.973
I'm a Christian,
I'm black, you know,

01:35:29.056 --> 01:35:32.309
I'm happy to wear
that complexity with pride.

01:35:32.393 --> 01:35:34.186
When people ask me
where I'm from,

01:35:34.228 --> 01:35:35.688
I say I'm from the Caribbean.

01:35:35.730 --> 01:35:38.065
Because like
I am and I'm very proud

01:35:38.149 --> 01:35:40.359
to be from the Caribbean.

01:35:40.401 --> 01:35:44.989
I am English and proudly English

01:35:45.072 --> 01:35:49.660
and I am Irish
and proudly Irish.

01:35:49.744 --> 01:35:51.454
Quintessentially British,

01:35:51.537 --> 01:35:52.955
I've never thought
of myself as English.

01:35:53.038 --> 01:35:54.623
I think we're a generation

01:35:54.707 --> 01:35:57.835
that have grown up
being Brits rather than...

01:35:57.918 --> 01:35:59.587
English in a way.

01:35:59.628 --> 01:36:02.089
If not more European.

01:36:02.131 --> 01:36:04.091
I am English,
I was born in England.

01:36:04.133 --> 01:36:06.552
But I also consider
myself British and to be honest,

01:36:06.594 --> 01:36:08.137
in the current
political climate,

01:36:08.220 --> 01:36:09.972
I consider myself European
as well.

01:36:10.055 --> 01:36:13.392
I'm not allowed to say
I consider myself European.

01:36:13.434 --> 01:36:15.936
Delete that bit from the--

01:36:18.314 --> 01:36:20.566
I consider myself British.

01:36:20.608 --> 01:36:22.234
I'm Lancashire
all the way through.

01:36:22.276 --> 01:36:23.903
I'm Chorley all the way through.

01:36:23.944 --> 01:36:26.947
We've got great sport,
we've got great food.

01:36:26.989 --> 01:36:31.076
Morecambe Bay Shrimps,
Bury Black Pudding,

01:36:31.118 --> 01:36:33.204
Uncle Joe's Mint Balls
from Wigan.

01:36:33.287 --> 01:36:35.122
With all this coming out
of Lancashire,

01:36:35.206 --> 01:36:36.415
we have so much to offer.

01:36:36.457 --> 01:36:37.917
So what do I call myself?

01:36:37.958 --> 01:36:39.418
A Lancastrian.

01:36:39.460 --> 01:36:41.253
Proud to be a Lancastrian,

01:36:41.295 --> 01:36:44.465
proud of my accent,
proud of the North of England.

01:36:44.507 --> 01:36:46.467
And of course, yes I'm English.

01:36:46.550 --> 01:36:48.093
But I'm British as well.

01:36:48.135 --> 01:36:50.721
And I represent a parliament

01:36:50.805 --> 01:36:54.183
that covers the United Kingdom
and that matters to me.

01:36:54.266 --> 01:36:57.978
So of course, yes,
I am British, I am English,

01:36:58.020 --> 01:37:00.648
but I'm a true Lancastrian.

01:37:00.689 --> 01:37:02.358
I was born in England.

01:37:02.441 --> 01:37:04.819
I have a deep attachment
to England.

01:37:04.860 --> 01:37:07.822
But I want to be part
of an internationalist future,

01:37:07.863 --> 01:37:10.282
not an English past.

01:37:10.324 --> 01:37:13.244
I spent a lot of my school days
and university time being taught

01:37:13.327 --> 01:37:16.455
that nationalism had
been a very bad, narrow thing

01:37:16.497 --> 01:37:18.207
which was now extinct.

01:37:18.290 --> 01:37:22.837
And yet we now see a new
brand of narrow Englishness

01:37:22.920 --> 01:37:24.672
that I think is terribly sad.

01:37:24.713 --> 01:37:28.509
And I think it should be
possible both to celebrate a,

01:37:28.551 --> 01:37:31.011
yes, quintessentially British

01:37:31.053 --> 01:37:33.597
or quintessentially English
occasion like this

01:37:33.681 --> 01:37:37.810
without wanting to take
the whole country

01:37:37.852 --> 01:37:40.354
into standing on
the white cliffs of Dover,

01:37:40.437 --> 01:37:42.982
giving two fingers
to Johnny Foreigner.

01:37:43.023 --> 01:37:44.733
Okay?

01:37:50.781 --> 01:37:52.658
If I go home and tell my wife

01:37:52.700 --> 01:37:54.368
that I've ended up
talking about spies,

01:37:54.410 --> 01:37:56.287
she'll just roll
her eyes completely,

01:37:56.370 --> 01:37:57.913
because that's one of my--

01:37:57.997 --> 01:38:00.082
I actually write spy fiction
in my spare time.

01:38:00.165 --> 01:38:03.168
[laughing]

01:38:03.210 --> 01:38:05.713
And it bores her to tears.

01:38:05.754 --> 01:38:08.424
My immediate predecessor
as Master of Pembroke,

01:38:08.507 --> 01:38:12.511
was Sir Richard Dearlove,
who had been head of MI6,

01:38:12.553 --> 01:38:17.516
known as C rather than M
in the James Bond films.

01:38:17.558 --> 01:38:20.561
So, yes, there have been people

01:38:20.603 --> 01:38:24.732
from the world of espionage
or counter-espionage here,

01:38:24.773 --> 01:38:28.652
but only in an official capacity
these days.

01:38:28.736 --> 01:38:32.740
We did have a previous Master
who was burned at the stake.

01:38:32.823 --> 01:38:34.825
I hope this isn't a pattern

01:38:34.909 --> 01:38:38.913
that will be repeated
for present and future Masters.

01:38:38.996 --> 01:38:40.748
I think Frank, it's gin o'clock,

01:38:40.789 --> 01:38:43.918
so it's been so nice
meeting you.

01:38:43.959 --> 01:38:48.589
And then this is what we have
very much enjoyed...

01:38:50.758 --> 01:38:53.761
creating, making,
and then drinking.

01:38:53.844 --> 01:38:56.472
Mind you,
it was four years of making

01:38:56.555 --> 01:38:59.016
and it was nine months
of tasting gin.

01:39:00.434 --> 01:39:02.186
FRANK MANNION:
Can you think

01:39:02.269 --> 01:39:04.313
of any Shakespearean expressions
off the top of your head?

01:39:04.396 --> 01:39:06.565
"Alas, poor Yorick!
I knew him well."

01:39:06.607 --> 01:39:09.026
Not me.
[laughing]

01:39:09.109 --> 01:39:11.028
-That's good, Fred, eh?
-Not bad.

01:39:11.111 --> 01:39:14.073
I'm educated, bruv.
London taxi driver, see?

01:39:14.114 --> 01:39:17.326
We know a bit about everything,
a bit about everything.

01:39:17.409 --> 01:39:19.620
I like to use,
"Therein lies the rub,"

01:39:19.662 --> 01:39:21.747
when I'm often making
a joke about something where,

01:39:21.789 --> 01:39:23.791
you know, it just
maybe doesn't work properly

01:39:23.832 --> 01:39:26.251
or whatever is not a big deal.
"Therein lies the rub."

01:39:26.293 --> 01:39:27.878
But of course,
you know, when it was used

01:39:27.962 --> 01:39:29.630
in a play originally,

01:39:29.672 --> 01:39:31.882
it was for a very,
very tragic situation, you know,

01:39:31.966 --> 01:39:33.384
probably something
life and death,

01:39:33.467 --> 01:39:35.052
it usually is in Shakespeare.

01:39:35.135 --> 01:39:38.305
"To be, or not to be,
that is the question."

01:39:38.389 --> 01:39:41.141
"Wherefore art thou Romeo?"

01:39:41.183 --> 01:39:45.229
"She who doth protest," you know
that sometimes gets said

01:39:45.312 --> 01:39:47.272
in a bit of a joking way
when you're like,

01:39:47.314 --> 01:39:49.984
"Well, I'm really not bothered
about this, but..."

01:39:50.067 --> 01:39:52.611
Personally,
I didn't like Shakespeare.

01:39:52.653 --> 01:39:54.488
You know,
the British have Shakespeare

01:39:54.530 --> 01:39:57.074
and us Chinese have Confucius.

01:39:57.157 --> 01:40:00.786
"Though this is madness,
there is method in it."

01:40:00.828 --> 01:40:03.247
"Such sweet sorrow."

01:40:03.330 --> 01:40:05.416
It's like, she sells
seashells on the seashore.

01:40:05.499 --> 01:40:07.876
The shells she sells
are seashells, I'm sure,

01:40:07.960 --> 01:40:11.588
but if she sells seashells
on the seashore,

01:40:11.672 --> 01:40:14.133
then I'm sure she
sells seashore shells

01:40:14.174 --> 01:40:17.386
and I bet
Ian McKellen can't do that.

01:40:20.347 --> 01:40:24.268
Shakespeare knew and said often
through his characters that,

01:40:24.351 --> 01:40:26.854
"All the world's a stage,

01:40:26.895 --> 01:40:29.398
"and all the men
and women merely players."

01:40:31.400 --> 01:40:33.277
We're all acting.

